#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# lane-start — the FIRST act of a lane: name the tmux window after the lane,
# put the lane's row in the register, and launch the session under the same
# name. One command at the boundary, so the three things that must agree are
# set in one act and cannot drift apart.
#
# THE LANE RULE (Brett Heap, 2026-09-10)
#   lane = tmux window name = Claude session name, in the form
#   `<repo>-<position across, left to right>`. The working directory confers
#   NO lane — two lanes may share one; a window still called `claude` has no
#   lane at all; and whether a name is free is decided by whether the
#   register row's recorded session id is LIVE, never by whether a row exists.
#
# PROTOCOL  ~/.agents/protocols/lane-collision-protocol.md
#   Rule 4 + Amendment 2 — the window is NAMED for the lane BEFORE the row is
#     appended, and peers are addressed by that name.
#   Rule 9 + Amendment 5 — every register write is one commit, made by
#     lanes-edit.sh, in the person's workspace repository.
#   Rule 10 — every row names its workstation.
#   Amendment 7 (DRAFT 2026-09-11) — after the row is written, the lane's own
#     object log records that the lane is here. Every line has an object, and
#     a lane-kind line's object is the lane itself:
#       STARTED — lane X, session <uuid>@<ws>, <UTC>, lane:X → home owner/repo; estate <estate>
#     `RESUMED` for a session that is being resumed rather than started. The
#     home repo comes from `git remote get-url origin` of the lane's OWN
#     directory: `<repo>-<n>` is a label, not a scope, and this line is the
#     only thing that says where a lane actually lives. It is resolved through
#     `lanes/repos.tsv` before it is written (R20), because a home is inherited
#     by every `#n` that lane writes and a legacy org spelling would key those
#     against a repository nothing else can reconcile them with.
#   Amendment 6 (DRAFT 2026-09-11, awaiting Brett Heap's word) — A LANE IS
#     RESUMED BY THE SESSION ID ITS ROW RECORDS, NEVER BY ITS TITLE. Titles are
#     not unique: `claude --help` says `-r, --resume [value]` is "Resume a
#     conversation by session ID, or open interactive picker with optional
#     search term", so only a session id is an exact resume — a title merely
#     filters the picker. On 2026-09-11 four transcripts in one working
#     directory carried the title `codeXfactory-1` and eight more carried
#     `codeXfactory-1 (2)` / `(4)`, while the session Brett was actually
#     driving carried no title at all.
#   Rule 3 + Amendment 8 (DRAFT 2026-09-12) — THE HANDOFF IS STAMPED HERE TOO.
#     On a resume, the handoff the row points at gets Rule 3's line, in place,
#     as the line after its header block:
#       RESUMED by <uuid> (lane <name>) at <UTC> — lane-start on <ws>
#     committed on its own as `handoff(<lane>@<ws>): RESUMED by <uuid>`, with
#     an explicit pathspec and a `Lane:` trailer (Amendment 4(d)), and pushed:
#     ONE `lanes-edit.sh write-handoff --stamp`, through the checkout every
#     write of the lane goes through and under its lock (Amendment 23, slice
#     2b). It NEVER refuses the launch: a stamp that cannot be written prints
#     the line and the command that writes it, and once the first register
#     write is made only a supervised restart's own fences stop a launch.
#     Seven resumes of one lane needed that
#     line written by hand, and while it was owed the counts drifted 6/4/5
#     across the row, the object log and the handoff itself.
#
# USAGE
#   lane-start [options] <repo> <n>      # lane-start openRepoShape 2
#   lane-start [options] <repo>-<n>      # the full lane name works too
#   lane-start [options] <n>             # <repo> = basename of the cwd's git root
#   lane-start --dir <path> <lane>       # any lane name, when --dir says where
#   lane-start                           # no <repo> and no <n>: this is `lane`
#
# BARE, THIS IS `lane` (Amendment 18 Addendum 1 clause (i-4)): the lanes,
# numbered, and the one you pick is bound. It renders nothing of its own — it
# `exec`s that word, carrying `--dry-run` and `--dir`, which mean the same thing
# there, and REFUSING by name the options `lane` has nothing to apply them to
# rather than dropping them. On a workstation with no `lane` the old refusal is
# still the whole answer.
#
# <repo> is parsed out of the lane name only because it is what locates the
# lane's directory. With `--dir` there is nothing left to parse, so the name is
# taken VERBATIM — which is how a lane named before the `<repo>-<n>` rule
# (`browser-ui-repair`) is started.
#
# OPTIONS
#   --dir <path>     the lane's checkout            (default $PROJECTS_ROOT/<repo>)
#   --estate <name>  the handoff estate folder      (default <repo>)
#   --dry-run        print every step; rename nothing, write nothing, launch
#                    nothing
#   --no-launch      do everything except the launch, and print the claude
#                    command on stdout — for the launcher integration and the
#                    tests
#   --confirm        when this window's name is not the lane, print the three
#                    facts that disagree — the WINDOW, the SESSION live in it,
#                    the ROW — and ask before taking it. The launcher passes it
#                    when the lane came from the swap RECORD rather than from
#                    the window name or a flag (Amendment 8)
#   --yes            answer that question yes without asking, for a caller that
#                    has already asked
#   --request-handoff  a lane bound in ANOTHER place is never taken (Amendment
#                    18(b)). This is the `y` to the question this command asks
#                    on a terminal, for a caller that has none: the request is
#                    written to the lane's log and pushed, typed into the bound
#                    pane where it is on this tmux server, and waited on
#   --force <why>    clause (e)'s override: release that binding ON ITS BEHALF
#                    and bind here. A SECOND invocation, never automatic, and
#                    the <why> is written into an append-only log and is what
#                    the bound session reads when it refuses every prompt from
#                    then on
#   --wait <s>       how long to wait for the handoff (default 300, clause (c))
#   --agent <name>   WHICH AGENT resumes this lane (Amendment 17(c)). Default:
#                    the agent of the lane's last `PAUSED` record, else
#                    `claude`. `claude` with its transcript here resumes it by
#                    id exactly as Amendment 6 has always done; every other
#                    case launches the named agent with the handoff's TOP BLOCK
#                    as its first prompt. The table is `claude` and `codex`; a
#                    name it does not carry is a refusal naming the two, never a
#                    guessed argv
#   --fresh          A NEW SESSION of this lane's agent, primed by the handoff's
#                    top block, whatever transcript the row names — the
#                    fresh-session mode as an ARGUMENT rather than as an
#                    environment variable (openRepoTools#94). The lane's own
#                    pending restart intent outranks it; a conflicting one is a
#                    refusal and never a substitution
#   --operation <id> the restart operation this launch belongs to, passed by the
#                    pane supervisor. The intent named by it is the one this run
#                    reads; an id that is not the lane's current operation is a
#                    refusal
#   --bind <uuid>    THE NAME GUARD'S BINDING MODE, and nobody else's: the
#                    guard passes it on a person's choice `2` at its offer
#                    (Amendment 12(h) rule 2, openRepoTools#70). <uuid> is the
#                    transcript of the session live in THIS window. It is
#                    appended to the lane's session cell even where another row
#                    records it — that `2` is the person's yes (clause (h) rule
#                    4) — and the row's stamp, the lane's log line and the
#                    handoff's Rule 3 stamp all name it, never the id this run
#                    would otherwise have resumed. It binds or it refuses: only
#                    with --no-launch, never with --fresh or --agent, and a
#                    <uuid> that is not this window's live session, or a window
#                    named for another lane, is a refusal before anything is
#                    written. IT IS IDEMPOTENT (feature 007, FR-039): where the
#                    lane's binding already names this window and <uuid>, and
#                    the session cell already ends on it, a rerun writes no
#                    register line again (not the row's state, not the lane's
#                    log line) and stamps the handoff only where it carries no
#                    `RESUMED by <uuid> (lane <L>)` line. A line that is there
#                    but not in origin's copy of the file (or whose copy cannot
#                    be read here) is still an owed stamp, and one note says so
#   -h, --help
#   --               everything after it is passed through to claude
#
# WHAT IT DOES, in order
#   1. derives LANE=<repo>-<n> and the lane's directory (which must exist);
#   2. refuses unless it is already inside tmux: it renames the window it is
#      run in and never creates a session;
#   3. LIVENESS CHECK, before it takes the name. If the register already has a
#      row for LANE it reads the session id(s) out of THE PUBLISHED row's
#      session cell — `lanes-edit.sh register-row`, which fetches and reads
#      `origin/<branch>` — and looks for a LIVE record (pid alive, start time
#      still matching, a status that is not `ended`) in every profile's
#      `sessions/*.json`. A live holder in ANOTHER tmux window is a refusal. A
#      row whose recorded sessions are all dead is a lane you are RESUMING, and
#      that is allowed;
#   3b. the LIVE SESSION IN THIS WINDOW (Amendment 8), which is not the same
#      question as step 3's. The harness mints a new transcript uuid with
#      nobody acting — a `/clear` does it, and so did the 2026-09-12 usage
#      reset that carried this lane's conversation into `4a91f1dc…` while its
#      row still ended on `09dd34d1…`. That id is in NO row, so step 3 cannot
#      see it and answers "this lane is parked" about the conversation sitting
#      in the very window it was asked about. Keyed on the WINDOW instead
#      (`lanes-edit.sh window-session`), the record is found: its uuid is
#      appended to the session cell in step 5, BEFORE the stamp, and it is what
#      this run resumes and stamps. There is no flag for it and there could not
#      be one — the id does not exist until the session does, so the act is
#      `lane-start --no-launch <repo> <n>` run IN that window, which is what the
#      SessionStart hook's block tells the new session to do. (`--bind <uuid>`
#      is the one caller already INSIDE that session — the name guard — naming
#      it, and it is the guard's alone: see OPTIONS);
#   3c. `--confirm`, if it was passed and this window is not already named for
#      the lane: the triple in front of a person, and a question. Before the
#      rename and before any write, so a No costs nothing;
#   4. `tmux rename-window <LANE>` — which also turns automatic-rename OFF for
#      that window (tmux(1) rename-window), so the name survives the next
#      command the window runs. A NEW lane's name is read back, and where
#      another window carries it too the rename is undone and the run refuses;
#   5. the row: `lanes-edit.sh add-row` when there is none, otherwise
#      `set-row-state`. Never a hand edit and never a whole-file write.
#      FEATURE 007 (T428): where the session is a transcript uuid this run
#      can name, the row (or its session cell and state) and the object log's
#      STARTED or RESUMED line are ONE write, `lanes-edit.sh start-record`,
#      one commit and push, made under this launch's act (`act-begin`, before
#      the first read) with the scopes its reads decided on;
#      THE STATE IS AMENDMENT 6(c)'s STAMP, written here rather than owed to
#      the session afterwards, and since Amendment 13(a) it is the WHOLE cell
#      (`<STATE> · <UTC> · <one line>`, the UTC stamped by the writer):
#        LIVE · <UTC> · RESUMED by <uuid> (lane <lane>) — lane-start on <ws>:
#        window renamed, launching <the command>; dir <dir>; window <ref>
#      The TAIL states what happened, so a `--no-launch` run ends `window
#      renamed, no launch` and never claims to have launched anything.
#      `STARTED` where a new session takes the name, and the uuid is the one
#      this run actually resumes or mints, read from the PUBLISHED row. A stamp
#      that is owed is a stamp that is missed, and under Amendment 6(b) a missed
#      stamp is not bookkeeping debt — it is a lost lane. A brand-new row needs
#      no stamp: it is created with that uuid already in its session cell;
#   6. the launch. It is DECIDED in step 5a, before the row is written, because
#      a brand-new session's id is minted there and step 5 must record it.
#      RESUME BEATS NEW throughout — a rename into a title that is still held
#      mints `<LANE> (2)` and the lane loses its address. Three cases, in order:
#        a. `exec claude --resume <id>`, where <id> is THE LAST session id in
#           the PUBLISHED row's session cell and
#           `<projects state>/<cwd key>/<id>.jsonl` exists. Exact: that
#           conversation and no other.
#        b. `exec claude --resume <LANE>` when no recorded id resolves but a
#           transcript here carries the custom title <LANE> — or `<LANE> (N)`,
#           which is the same lane: that suffix is what a rename into a title
#           something else still holds mints, and the liveness check has always
#           read it that way. THE FALLBACK, not the rule — it opens the picker
#           filtered by the title, and several transcripts can share one title.
#           A suffix found here is reported: it means a stale holder was live,
#           and the cure is to retire the holders, not to live with `(4)`.
#        c. `exec claude --name <LANE> --session-id <fresh uuid>` for a lane
#           with no history here. The uuid is minted so the row can carry it,
#           which is what makes (a) work on the next start.
#
# THE RESUME TARGET — WHY THE LAST ID, AND WHY THE PUBLISHED CELL
#   The session cell is a HISTORY, written left to right: a lane's row reads
#   `<first> → <after the first /clear> → <after the profile switch>`, oldest
#   first, because each succeeding session appends itself. So the LAST session
#   id in the cell is the lane's current one, and that is the only id this
#   script will resume. Earlier ids are the lane's past and are never resumed
#   (a stale lineage is precisely what went wrong on 2026-09-11).
#
#   AND THE CELL IS THE PUBLISHED ONE. `$LANES_FILE` is this checkout's working
#   tree, and a `git fetch` does not move a working tree: a uuid a peer stamped
#   from another clone and pushed is simply absent here until something pulls.
#   Read from there, a row published as `<U1> → <U2>` still reads `<U1>` — so
#   the operator lands in the lane's PREVIOUS conversation, the status appended
#   to the row names the wrong id, and the RESUMED line goes into
#   `lanes/log/<lane>.md` carrying a session uuid that is not the lane's
#   current one, in a file nothing ever rewrites. Every other state read in
#   this tooling goes through `origin/<branch>` after a fetch; this one does
#   too, through the same `register-row` this script already asks before it
#   adds a row. `live-holder`'s id set stays a UNION of both (R25) and is the
#   one exception, for the opposite reason: liveness fails CLOSED, so an id
#   only this checkout knows is one more reason to refuse a rename.
#
# KEEPING THE ROW CURRENT
#   Resuming by a recorded id only works if the row is current, so this script
#   makes it current where it can:
#     * case (c), NEW ROW — the row is created with the minted id already in
#       its session cell, instead of `pending — set by the session's first act`;
#     * case (c), EXISTING ROW — `lanes-edit.sh append-session-id` appends
#       `→ harness <minted id>` after the cell's last id, matching that id
#       INSIDE THE SESSION CELL: the 6(c) stamp writes the same uuid twice more
#       into the state cell, so a whole-row anchor (`replace-in-row`) refuses on
#       every row this tool has already stamped — three occurrences in this
#       estate's own `openRepoProject-1` row. When the cell has no
#       id to append to (some rows record only `session_…` footer ids, which
#       are not transcript ids), it prints the command and leaves the act to
#       the session — it never guesses at the cell's text;
#     * case (a) — nothing to do: the row already names the session being
#       resumed;
#     * case (b) — the id cannot be known before the picker runs, so the
#       session stamps its own row as its first act (Amendment 6(c)).
#   `--session-id` is set to LANE_START_SESSION_ID=0 to turn the minting off;
#   the row then reads `pending — set by the session's first act` as before.
#
# WHAT COUNTS AS A SESSION ID IN THE CELL
#   The TRANSCRIPT uuid — the name of the session's `<uuid>.jsonl` — and only
#   that, because it is the only id `--resume` takes and the only one the
#   liveness records key on. Some rows record a PR-FOOTER id (`session_01…`)
#   instead; those cannot be resumed or checked, so the script names them and
#   says the row needs the transcript uuid rather than treating the cell as
#   empty. Every RESUMED / handoff / takeover stamp writes the transcript uuid.
#
# OUTPUT  every step and every refusal goes to stderr; the ONLY thing written
#   to stdout is `--no-launch`'s command line, so `--no-launch` composes.
#
# EXIT
#   0  done
#   1  environment — not inside tmux, no tmux, a RECORDED directory that is
#      gone, no register, or `lanes-edit.sh live-holder` failed (which is NOT
#      the same as its answering 8, "no live session holds this lane").
#      And, since feature 007 (T428; lane 3's A2, #165 6774499606), a base
#      this launch could not take: `act-begin` exiting 2 (a helper with no
#      `start-record`), 1, 64 or any code but 0, 69, 75 and 3, or exiting 0
#      with no line or a malformed one; `act-begin --rebase` the same, its 2
#      being one of Y:5595's three refusals of its own (a malformed
#      `LANES_ACT`, a `files` base on primary, or a changed generation),
#      with its words kept (#165 6779688808, Q23; REV-ACT2 N3); or a failed
#      `canon-lane --walk`: any code but 0, 69, 75 and 3 (its 64, a helper
#      with no `--walk`; its 66, an alias table it could not read), or an
#      exit 0 that prints no name; or `act-begin` run again after a resume's
#      `start-record` 2 under an `@files` base (ERR-56), the same, after
#      which this window's rename is undone. Each
#      names the verb and its code. Nothing is renamed or written, save the
#      `request-handoff` line a `--rebase` follows, which stands.
#   2  refusal — an ARGUMENT ERROR, and one further case this amendment adds: a
#      name that is not a lane, a position that is not a position, a name a
#      window cannot take, a lane whose register holds two rows and so resolves
#      to none, a lane that is live in another window — and A LANE WHOSE
#      CHECKOUT IS NOT RECORDED AND CANNOT BE PROVED (Evidence 7).
#
#      THAT LAST ONE MOVED FROM 1 TO 2 AND THE MOVE IS THE POINT. Evidence 7:
#      a lane whose record predates clause (c) carries no `dir`, the default
#      `$PROJECTS_ROOT/<repo>` did not exist, and this script exited **1**
#      behind the launcher's `exec` — a pane that said `[exited]` with the
#      message scrolled past it. It is a REFUSAL a person fixes with one flag,
#      not an environment that failed, and `restart` already spends 2 on the
#      same fact (clause (i), `R-A11-20`: *"no such lane, no recorded
#      directory, no profile recorded yet"*). A directory the lane itself
#      RECORDED and which is now gone keeps 1: that is a path that was right
#      and moved, and nothing is searched for in its place.
#
#      A PERSON'S ANSWER IS NOT AN ARGUMENT ERROR. `--confirm` answered `N`, and
#      `--confirm` with no tty and no `--yes` — the same answer with nobody
#      there to give it — rename nothing, write nothing, print one notice and
#      `exec` Claude BARE with the pass-through arguments, exit 0. A launcher
#      that `exec`s this script would otherwise leave the operator in an exited
#      pane with no Claude in it. Under `--no-launch` the bare command is
#      printed on stdout, so a launcher can exec it itself.
#   69, 75, 3  a register read this command cannot do without, refused with
#      the reading verb's own code (feature 007, T402; FR-036): 69, the
#      register could not be read; 75, register busy; 3, in doubt. This
#      launch has written no row, no STARTED or RESUMED line and no stamp,
#      and launched nothing; a refusal after this window's rename gives it
#      its name back. One line of the handoff may stand: where a
#      `lane-transcript` read refuses after `request-handoff` answered 0, the
#      HANDOFF-REQUESTED line or the forced PAUSED it wrote stands, written
#      whole to the append-only log (R-A11-11), and the refusal names it. The
#      default read arms see these codes only from a SQL register;
#      `register-text`, the read of the lane's row, spends 69 in every mode
#      where the register cannot be read, and any code it exits with is passed
#      on, never read as an empty register. The ownership seam's read,
#      `managed-projection`, is the exception: it exits 1, UNKNOWN, for these
#      codes too (research.md ERR-46).
#      AND THE ACT'S WRITES (feature 007, T428; spec:317-318): `act-begin`'s
#      or `canon-lane --walk`'s 69, 75 or 3 where this launch could take no
#      base or know no walk, or could not read its base again after an
#      `@files` 2 (every other code is 1, above); `start-record`'s 2 under a
#      seq base, which on primary is a second binding, in every case, and
#      under `@files` where it makes a new lane's row, binds under `--bind`,
#      or `act-begin`, run again, answers a seq base (ERR-56; lane 3, #165
#      6867981064); its code where it makes a new lane's row, or binds under
#      `--bind`; and `request-handoff`'s 69, 75 and 3. A resume's refused
#      `start-record` is a note, and the launch goes on, its 2 included under
#      `@files` (Amendment 23 S3); so is a refused W2 to W6, which run as
#      their own verbs where no transcript uuid is known. Under a seq base
#      that note,
#      after any code but 3, prints no rerun of its own and names `lane-start
#      --no-launch`, run in the launched session's window as its first act
#      (lane 3's Q39 and Q41, #165 6780753157). With a uuid known, that act
#      has this run's own arguments: its lane words, `--dir`, `--estate`,
#      `--agent` and `--bind`, as typed, and no other option, nor `--` and
#      what follows it (Q64, #165 6807881675). With none, it is Amendment
#      7(b)'s line, the lane and the `--agent` it was given, which the
#      deferred-record note prints too (Q65, #165 6808141050). Where the act
#      dies instead, on a new row's code but 3 and on `request-handoff`'s 2,
#      it says to run `lane-start` again from its start (Q63, Q66).
#   130, 143  stopped by INT or TERM (128 plus the signal), in lane-end's form
#      (REV-ACT2 N2): the run ends where the signal found it, removes its
#      temporary files, and never resumes. A verb it was waiting on ends
#      first, by its own rules, and its code is not read; what was written
#      before stands, and nothing more is written or launched. A ^C or a TERM
#      at either `[y/N]` prompt, `--confirm`'s or the one asked before a
#      `request-handoff`, ends the run there, with nothing renamed or
#      written: a signal is not an answer, so R-A8-3's "no answer exits" is
#      not broken by it (the comment at the first prompt says why).
#
# ENVIRONMENT
#   PROJECTS_ROOT        default ~/projects
#   LANES_FILE           default <the workspace repository>/lanes/LANES.md
#   LANES_EDIT           default lanes-edit.sh beside this command
#   AGENT_PROTOCOL_ROOT  dir holding workspace.yaml (default ~/.agents)
#   OPENREPOTOOLS_BIN_DIR  where `--install` put the commands (default
#                        ~/.local/bin) — searched for lanes-edit.sh
#   CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR    the profile whose sessions/ and projects/ are read
#   CLAUDE_PROJECTS_DIR  default $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/projects, else ~/.claude/projects
#   CLAUDE_BIN           the Claude Code to launch. Unset, it is the absolute
#                        path `claude-current --porcelain` answers, the one
#                        beside this command or in $OPENREPOTOOLS_BIN_DIR and
#                        never one on PATH: npm's published version, updated
#                        first when every copy is behind, and a stale one
#                        REFUSED with exit 2 before anything is written
#                        (opensoft/workBenches#119). `claude` from PATH only
#                        where no claude-current is installed. Set, it is a pin
#                        and launched as it is, unless it equals
#                        CLAUDE_RESOLVED_BIN with no CLAUDE_VERIFIED_VERSION:
#                        that is an earlier launch's answer inherited through a
#                        session's environment, and it is resolved again
#   CLAUDE_RESOLVED_BIN  the path a resolution chose, exported beside CLAUDE_BIN
#   CLAUDE_VERIFIED_VERSION  the version a launcher's own claude-current read
#                        for CLAUDE_BIN (equal to CLAUDE_RESOLVED_BIN): recorded
#                        as `claude <version>` on the log line, and removed
#                        before the launch so no session inherits it
#   CLAUDE_ALLOW_STALE=1 claude-current answers with a stale copy instead of
#                        refusing it; docs/README-claude-current.md has the rest
#   LANE_START_SESSION_ID  0 turns off minting a uuid for a new session (and
#                        therefore turns off writing it into the row)
#   LANE_START_HANDOFF_ANSWER  answers Amendment 18(c)'s question — *"ask it to
#                        hand off, then bind here?"* — without a terminal. The
#                        seam the tests drive it through, and its own variable
#                        rather than `LANE_START_ANSWER`'s because one run can
#                        ask both questions and a single seam would answer the
#                        wrong one. Set at all, it is the answer; anything but
#                        y/yes is No
#   LANE_START_ANSWER    answers `--confirm`'s question without a terminal —
#                        the seam the tests drive it through. Set at all, it is
#                        the answer; anything but y/yes is No
#   LANES_REPO           the checkout the register and the handoffs share
#                        (default: the workspace repository)
#   LANES_ROUTE_WRITES=1 Amendment 23's opt-in: with neither LANES_REPO nor
#                        LANES_FILE set, every write of the lane goes through
#                        its own register checkout <W>-lanes/<lane>/, made at
#                        "5. the row", and W, the workspace repository, is
#                        fast-forwarded (`lanes-edit.sh refresh-workspace`)
#                        before the row is read and again before the launch
#   LANES_WORKSPACE_ROOT the workspace repository, overriding the `path:` in
#                        ${AGENT_PROTOCOL_ROOT:-~/.agents}/workspace.yaml — the
#                        test seam, and the only override there is
#   LANES_BRANCH         default main — the register's branch: what every write
#                        pushes, and what W is fast-forwarded to
#   LANES_GIT_TIMEOUT    seconds bounding each git call `lanes-edit.sh` makes
#                        over the NETWORK (default 60; 0 or a non-number turns
#                        the bound off). This command makes none of its own
#   LANES_GIT            the git binary THOSE calls use, so a test can hand them
#                        a git that hangs without shadowing git for the script
#   LANES_NO_GIT=1       write the handoff's stamp but commit nothing
#   LANE_START_FRESH=1   a NEW session of this lane's agent, started with the
#                        top block of the handoff the row names as its first
#                        prompt — which is what a context clear IS. It was
#                        `/ctx`'s one seam under Amendment 17(f) and it still
#                        works; it is NO LONGER THE AUTHORITY (openRepoTools#94):
#                        the lane's own restart intent outranks it and `--fresh`
#                        stands beside it, because an environment variable does
#                        not survive the launcher's `tmux new-session` and this
#                        command was measured being reached without it
#
# It spells no absolute path of its own. Since Amendment 9(a) the register is
# found through `$AGENT_PROTOCOL_ROOT/workspace.yaml` rather than beside this
# file — this is an installed command now, and a command's own directory is
# evidence about the command, not about a person's data. Its WRITER is found
# beside it, because `--install` places the two together. Everything else hangs
# off $HOME.

set -euo pipefail

# AN INHERITED `LANES_READ_MIRROR` IS NO ANSWER (feature 007, T402; lane 3's #165
# 6861148117: ERR-30 widens PER CALL; lane 5's #165 6861222075). This command
# names the variable on no call of its own. Under T504, which marks `lane-last`
# for every caller, a value the caller exported would lower the RETIRED check's
# `lane-last` read, which decides, to the mirror where an outage must end it 69,
# and it lowers every other marked read this command makes. So it is dropped
# here, before any read and before anything that could pass the environment on,
# as `lane-handoff` does at its entry.
unset LANES_READ_MIRROR

prog=lane-start

# FEATURE 007, T428: `LANES_EXPECT` IS NEVER EXPORTED (Y:5581). An act's
# scopes ride as a prefix on the one write they belong to, so one this
# command inherited was written for nothing it calls. It is removed before
# the first call, not at the act's base: until then `refresh-workspace`,
# the `lanes --prefix` listing and the `lane` that a bare `lane-start`
# `exec`s all ran with a caller's scopes (W11-T428, the probe for lane 2's
# note, #165 6812474015).
unset LANES_EXPECT

SELF="${BASH_SOURCE[0]}"
RESOLVED="$(readlink -f -- "$SELF" 2>/dev/null || printf '%s' "$SELF")"
SCRIPT_DIR="$(cd -- "$(dirname -- "$RESOLVED")" && pwd)"

# --- THE WORKSPACE REPOSITORY (lane-collision-protocol Amendment 9(a)) -------
#
# The person's data — the register `lanes/LANES.md`, the object logs
# `lanes/log/`, the handoffs, the manifests `park` writes, and a
# `lanes/repos.tsv` override — is found through
# `$AGENT_PROTOCOL_ROOT/workspace.yaml`, and NEVER from this file's own
# location. `resume:466` and `status:1673` already read that same file under
# that same name, and Amendment 9(a) mints no second one: a second way to find
# it is a second answer.
#
# Deriving it from here stopped being POSSIBLE the moment this became an
# installed regular file in `~/.local/bin` with no register anywhere near it,
# and it stopped being WANTED for a subtler reason: a file's location on disk
# is evidence about the file, not about a person's data.
#
# THESE FOUR FUNCTIONS ARE A DELIBERATE COPY, byte for byte, across
# `lanes-edit.sh`, `lane-start`, `lane-end` and `link-estates` — the same trade
# openRepoTools already makes for its own fetch shim (`openRepoTools:93-100`).
# A shared `lanes-common.sh` would be a TENTH file for `--install` to place and
# a broken helper the first time somebody copied only some of them. Each of
# these is one file a person has on PATH.
#
# FAILING TO FIND IT IS A REFUSAL, NEVER A GUESS. No file, no `path:`, no
# `repository:`, or a `path:` that is not the root of a checkout of the
# `repository:` named beside it → the caller refuses, exit 1 — the code
# `lanes-edit.sh` has always used for *registry not found* — with the fix
# named: `openRepoTools wip init`. Nothing here ever creates that file, writes
# a register, or falls back to a path derived from its own location. THE
# CHECKOUT TEST IS NEW: nothing validated it before this, so a `path:` pointing
# at some other repository used to be accepted and now is not.
#
# `LANES_WORKSPACE_ROOT` is the test seam and the only override — the seam
# `lane-start` already spelled for handoff resolution, widened to the whole
# question so `test_lane_helpers.sh` can point every helper at one sandbox.
# --- BEGIN shared workspace resolver (Amendment 9(a)) ----------------------
# BYTE-IDENTICAL IN ALL FOUR LANE HELPERS, and held there by
# `tests/test_repo_hygiene.py`. The estate commands carry the same discipline
# for their own estate resolver and for the same reason its guard gives: a
# drifted copy is TWO ANSWERS to one question, and "where is this person's
# workspace" is exactly the question Amendment 9(a) exists to give one answer
# to. Edit it in one file and the test names the other three.
LANES_WS_WHY=""

lanes_ws_yaml() { printf '%s\n' "${AGENT_PROTOCOL_ROOT:-$HOME/.agents}/workspace.yaml"; }

# `key: value` at the TOP LEVEL only, first occurrence, with an inline `#`
# comment and surrounding quotes stripped and a leading `~` expanded against
# $HOME (nothing expands a bare `~` inside a file). The anchored `^key:` never
# reaches into the INDENTED `orgs:` map, which Amendment 9(a) puts out of
# scope: these helpers read `repository:` and `path:` and nothing else, because
# one register in the first workspace repository is the whole rule — the
# register never follows that map. Two lines of sed, because a YAML parser is
# not a dependency any of these files will take for one scalar.
lanes_ws_field() {
  local v
  v="$(sed -n -e "s/^$1:[[:space:]]*//p" "$2" 2>/dev/null | head -n 1 |
    sed -e 's/[[:space:]]*#.*$//' -e 's/[[:space:]]*$//')"
  v="${v%\"}"; v="${v#\"}"
  v="${v%\'}"; v="${v#\'}"
  case "$v" in
  '~') v="$HOME" ;;
  '~/'*) v="$HOME/${v#'~/'}" ;;
  esac
  printf '%s\n' "$v"
}

# THE WORKSTATION'S NAME IS READ, NEVER INVENTED — AND NEVER `hostname` INSIDE A
# CONTAINER (lane-collision-protocol Amendment 11, ratified decision 8(d), as
# corrected by A11 Addendum 3's `R-A11-14`).
#
# WHAT WENT WRONG. `opensoft/brett-wip` `origin/main` carries SIX lines in
# `lanes/log/openRepoProject-1.md` whose workstation is a container id — five
# `@0e7d1a79a07e`, one `@55cceb3e5bc9` — in an append-only file, written by every
# writer inside that container and not only by Evidence 6's fork. A row on a
# workstation that does not exist is a row no reader can match, no crossing can
# detect and no `swapped <ws>` can answer for; and the log is never rewritten, so
# each one is wrong for ever.
#
# WHO OWNS THE VALUE. `R-A11-14`: the **workBenches launcher**, which runs on the
# host and exports `LANES_WORKSTATION=<the host's own hostname -s>` into every
# session AND every container it starts, beside the `CLAUDE_PROFILE_NAME` it
# already exports. These helpers READ it. They do not write it, they do not
# derive it from a second file, and THEY NEVER SUBSTITUTE A PLACEHOLDER: an
# `@unknown-workstation` is the same defect one field along from the `unknown`
# clause (e) refuses in the session field, and the rejected alternative — a
# `workstation:` key in `workspace.yaml` — is a SECOND PLACE FOR THE TRUTH TO BE
# WRONG beside the variable the launcher already sets.
#
# THE THREE ANSWERS, as `<name><TAB><source>`:
#
#   seam              `$LANES_WORKSTATION` is set. This is the ordinary case
#                     wherever the launcher started the session.
#   hostname          no seam, and this is NOT a container: `hostname -s`, which
#                     is what the seam has defaulted to since Amendment 6 and
#                     what every row written before this rung was written by.
#   container-unset   no seam, and this IS a container. The NAME still answers,
#                     because a read in front of every launch may not refuse —
#                     it will simply match nothing, which is the honest answer —
#                     but every WRITER refuses, exit 2, naming the variable and
#                     the launcher that sets it.
#
# IT ANSWERS AS A PAIR, AND THAT IS NOT A STYLE CHOICE. A caller reads the name
# with `$(lanes_workstation)`, which is a COMMAND SUBSTITUTION and therefore a
# subshell: a global the function set there dies with it. The source is on the
# same line as the name, which is the only way out of a subshell there is.
#
#   $LANES_IN_CONTAINER   1 or 0, forcing the probe. THE TEST SEAM, and nothing
#                         else sets it: the suite must be able to exercise both
#                         halves of this rule on one host, and the host it runs
#                         on is a container exactly when it is.
lanes_in_container() {
  case "${LANES_IN_CONTAINER:-}" in
  1) return 0 ;;
  0) return 1 ;;
  esac
  [ -e /.dockerenv ] || [ -e /run/.containerenv ]
}

lanes_workstation_pair() {
  local v
  if [ -n "${LANES_WORKSTATION:-}" ]; then
    printf '%s\t%s\n' "$LANES_WORKSTATION" seam
    return 0
  fi
  v="$(hostname -s 2>/dev/null || hostname 2>/dev/null || printf 'unknown')"
  if lanes_in_container; then
    printf '%s\t%s\n' "$v" container-unset
  else
    printf '%s\t%s\n' "$v" hostname
  fi
}

# THE NAME ALONE, which is what every writer of the workstation column wants.
lanes_workstation() { lanes_workstation_pair | cut -f1; }

# The ONE SENTENCE a writer refuses with, in one place, so the three writers
# cannot word it three ways. Empty on every other source, so
# `[ -n "$(lanes_workstation_why "$src")" ]` is the whole of the test.
lanes_workstation_why() {   # <source>
  [ "${1:-}" = container-unset ] || return 0
  printf '%s\n' "this is a container and \$LANES_WORKSTATION is not set, so there is no workstation name to write. This host's own \`hostname\` is the CONTAINER'S id, not a workstation, and a row on a workstation that does not exist is a row no reader can match — in a log nothing ever rewrites (Amendment 11, decision 8(d); R-A11-14). The value is the workBenches launcher's to export into every session and container it starts. Set it for this one and re-run: export LANES_WORKSTATION=<this host's name>"
}

# One of the spellings `git remote get-url origin` prints for one GitHub
# repository, reduced to a bare lowercased `<owner>/<name>` so it can be
# compared against workspace.yaml's own `<owner>/<name>`. A URL that is not
# GitHub's is compared as it stands, lowercased — which is what lets a sandbox
# name a bare local origin and still be checked.
lanes_ws_norm() {
  local u="${1%.git}"
  case "$u" in
  https://github.com/*) u="${u#https://github.com/}" ;;
  ssh://git@github.com/*) u="${u#ssh://git@github.com/}" ;;
  git@github.com:*) u="${u#git@github.com:}" ;;
  esac
  printf '%s' "$u" | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]'
}

# Do these two name one repository? Equal after normalisation, OR the origin's
# path ENDS in the `<owner>/<name>` the pointer file spells — which is how a
# GitHub Enterprise host, an ssh alias and a local bare mirror laid out by
# owner and name all say the same thing as `https://github.com/<owner>/<name>`.
# The pointer file spells `<owner>/<name>` because that is what a person types;
# `git remote get-url` spells a URL because that is what git stores.
lanes_ws_same_repo() {
  local a b
  a="$(lanes_ws_norm "$1")"
  b="$(lanes_ws_norm "$2")"
  [ -n "$a" ] && [ -n "$b" ] || return 1
  [ "$a" = "$b" ] && return 0
  case "$a" in */"$b") return 0 ;; esac
  return 1
}

# Prints the workspace checkout's real root, or returns 1 having set
# $LANES_WS_WHY to the one sentence that says which of the five ways it failed.
# ROOT-NESS IS ITS OWN CHECK: `rev-parse --is-inside-work-tree` and
# `remote get-url origin` both succeed from any SUBDIRECTORY of a work tree, so
# a `path:` naming a subdirectory would otherwise be accepted and resolve
# `<path>/lanes/LANES.md` inside the wrong directory.
lanes_workspace_root() {
  local yaml repo path origin toplevel real
  LANES_WS_WHY=""
  if [ -n "${LANES_WORKSPACE_ROOT:-}" ]; then
    printf '%s\n' "$LANES_WORKSPACE_ROOT"
    return 0
  fi
  yaml="$(lanes_ws_yaml)"
  if [ ! -r "$yaml" ]; then
    LANES_WS_WHY="there is no $yaml"
    return 1
  fi
  repo="$(lanes_ws_field repository "$yaml")"
  path="$(lanes_ws_field path "$yaml")"
  if [ -z "$repo" ]; then
    LANES_WS_WHY="$yaml names no \`repository:\`"
    return 1
  fi
  if [ -z "$path" ]; then
    LANES_WS_WHY="$yaml names no \`path:\`"
    return 1
  fi
  if ! git -C "$path" rev-parse --is-inside-work-tree >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    LANES_WS_WHY="$yaml names path: $path, which is not a git checkout"
    return 1
  fi
  origin="$(git -C "$path" remote get-url origin 2>/dev/null || :)"
  if ! lanes_ws_same_repo "$origin" "$repo"; then
    LANES_WS_WHY="$yaml names repository: $repo, but $path is a checkout of ${origin:-no origin at all}"
    return 1
  fi
  toplevel="$(git -C "$path" rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null || :)"
  real="$(cd -- "$path" 2>/dev/null && pwd -P)" || real=""
  if [ -z "$real" ] || [ "$toplevel" != "$real" ]; then
    LANES_WS_WHY="$yaml names path: $path, which is not the ROOT of that checkout (${toplevel:-unknown} is)"
    return 1
  fi
  printf '%s\n' "$real"
}

# The refusal's body, so all four helpers say the same thing in the same words.
#
# THE REASON IS RE-DERIVED HERE, IN THE PARENT SHELL, and that is the whole
# point of this line. Every caller reaches the resolver through a command
# substitution — `LANES_WS_ROOT="$(lanes_workspace_root || :)"` — which runs it
# in a SUBSHELL, so the $LANES_WS_WHY it set died with that subshell and the
# parent still holds the empty string it started with. Six distinct diagnoses
# were being computed and all six were being thrown away; every one of the
# refusals Amendment 9(a) enumerates printed the same "no reason recorded".
# The resolver is read-only and cheap — two `sed`s and three `git` reads — and
# it is reached on the refusal path only, so running it again HERE is the one
# place its answer survives to be printed.
lanes_workspace_why() {
  [ -n "$LANES_WS_WHY" ] || lanes_workspace_root >/dev/null 2>&1 || :
  printf '%s' "the workspace repository could not be found — ${LANES_WS_WHY:-no reason recorded}.
    Amendment 9(a): every lane helper reads \`repository:\` and \`path:\` from
    $(lanes_ws_yaml), never from its own location. One command writes that
    file, creates or clones the repository and places the symlinks:
        openRepoTools wip init"
}
# --- END shared workspace resolver ------------------------------------------

# THE REGISTER AND ITS WRITER, after the move (Amendment 9(b)). The register is
# the workspace repository's `lanes/LANES.md`, found through the pointer file
# above. The writer is `lanes-edit.sh`, which `--install` places BESIDE this
# command — in `~/.local/bin` when installed, in the checkout when run from one
# — so `$SCRIPT_DIR` answers both without a second rule. `$OPENREPOTOOLS_BIN_DIR`
# and `$PATH` are the fallbacks for a command copied somewhere on its own.
LANES_WS_ROOT=""
# AMENDMENT 23 (act 2, slice 1a). With neither variable set, every write of a
# lane goes through that lane's own register checkout, and the register this
# command READS follows it there once the lane's name is known
# (`lane_register_follow`, below). A caller's `LANES_REPO` without `LANES_FILE`
# is the checkout the helper now writes in, so its own register is the one read.
LANES_REG_DEFAULT=0
[ -n "${LANES_FILE:-}" ] || [ -n "${LANES_REPO:-}" ] || LANES_REG_DEFAULT=1
if [ -z "${LANES_FILE:-}" ] || [ -z "${LANES_REPO:-}" ]; then
  LANES_WS_ROOT="$(lanes_workspace_root || :)"
fi
: "${LANES_REPO:=$LANES_WS_ROOT}"
LANES_FILE="${LANES_FILE:-${LANES_REPO:+$LANES_REPO/lanes/LANES.md}}"

lanes_edit_default() {
  local d
  [ -x "$SCRIPT_DIR/lanes-edit.sh" ] && { printf '%s\n' "$SCRIPT_DIR/lanes-edit.sh"; return 0; }
  d="${OPENREPOTOOLS_BIN_DIR:-$HOME/.local/bin}"
  [ -x "$d/lanes-edit.sh" ] && { printf '%s\n' "$d/lanes-edit.sh"; return 0; }
  command -v lanes-edit.sh 2>/dev/null && return 0
  printf '%s\n' "$SCRIPT_DIR/lanes-edit.sh"
}
LANES_EDIT="${LANES_EDIT:-$(lanes_edit_default)}"

PROJECTS_ROOT="${PROJECTS_ROOT:-$HOME/projects}"
CLAUDE_HOME="${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:-$HOME/.claude}"
PROJECTS_STATE="${CLAUDE_PROJECTS_DIR:-$CLAUDE_HOME/projects}"

# WHICH CLAUDE CODE A LAUNCH STARTS (opensoft/workBenches#119, Brett Heap
# 2026-09-29, verbatim "for 2, we can run update on every start, this ensures
# we have the latest models"). Decided here, from the environment as it
# arrived, and acted on by `lane_claude_resolve_launch` below, once a launch
# is certain: before the binding handoff, and in the bare launch a `--confirm`
# answered No takes. Three shapes, and docs/README-claude-current.md has the
# contract:
#   * CLAUDE_BIN unset: resolve through `claude-current`, the one `--install`
#     places beside this command;
#   * CLAUDE_BIN equal to CLAUDE_RESOLVED_BIN with no CLAUDE_VERIFIED_VERSION:
#     an earlier launch's answer, inherited through a session's environment.
#     It is not an operator's pin, so it is resolved again;
#   * anything else is a pin and is launched as it is. A pin that a launcher's
#     own `claude-current` produced carries CLAUDE_VERIFIED_VERSION, which is
#     recorded on the log line as `claude <version>`.
# CLAUDE_VERIFIED_VERSION is read once and removed, so it never reaches the
# session: a launch from inside that session must resolve again.
lane_claude_version=""
lane_claude_resolve=0
lane_claude_checked=0
if [ -z "${CLAUDE_BIN:-}" ]; then
  lane_claude_resolve=1
elif [ "$CLAUDE_BIN" = "${CLAUDE_RESOLVED_BIN:-}" ]; then
  if [ -n "${CLAUDE_VERIFIED_VERSION:-}" ]; then
    lane_claude_version="$CLAUDE_VERIFIED_VERSION"
  else
    lane_claude_resolve=1
  fi
fi
unset CLAUDE_VERIFIED_VERSION
CLAUDE_BIN="${CLAUDE_BIN:-claude}"

# `claude-current` is looked for where `--install` puts it and NEVER on PATH:
# beside this command, else in $OPENREPOTOOLS_BIN_DIR. A sandbox that copies
# this command somewhere without it, as the lane suite does, then launches the
# `claude` its own PATH names, and no test reaches a real npm.
lane_claude_current() {
  local d
  [ -x "$SCRIPT_DIR/claude-current" ] && { printf '%s\n' "$SCRIPT_DIR/claude-current"; return 0; }
  d="${OPENREPOTOOLS_BIN_DIR:-$HOME/.local/bin}"
  [ -x "$d/claude-current" ] && { printf '%s\n' "$d/claude-current"; return 0; }
  return 1
}

# lane_claude_resolve_launch: for a run that is to resolve (above) and is about
# to launch, `claude-current --porcelain` names the Claude Code to start. It
# reads npm's published version, updates the user-writable install first when
# every copy is behind, and answers with an absolute path it has run
# `--version` on; CLAUDE_BIN becomes that path, exported with
# CLAUDE_RESOLVED_BIN. Its exit 2 is a refusal (every copy still behind npm)
# and ends this run with 2; any other failure (1, nothing runnable; 64, a
# malformed setting) ends it with 1, the environment status. Both callers come
# before a binding handoff, window rename or transcript move, and the bare
# launch a `--confirm` answered No
# takes. Its stderr is the operator's and goes straight through. Never under
# `--no-launch`, which launches nothing and is the first act INSIDE a running
# session, and never under `--dry-run`, which must not update anything. It
# resolves once: a second call does nothing.
lane_claude_resolve_launch() {
  local lcc_cmd lcc_out lcc_rc=0 lcc_line lcc_path="" lcc_version="" lcc_status=""
  (( lane_claude_resolve )) || return 0
  (( ! no_launch )) || return 0
  lane_claude_resolve=0
  if ! lcc_cmd="$(lane_claude_current)"; then
    note "no claude-current beside this command or in ${OPENREPOTOOLS_BIN_DIR:-$HOME/.local/bin}, so $CLAUDE_BIN is launched as it is (through PATH, where it is a bare name), unchecked against npm; openRepoTools --install places claude-current"
    return 0
  fi
  if (( dry_run )); then
    plan "$lcc_cmd --porcelain   (chooses the Claude Code to launch; not run under --dry-run, because it may update one)"
    return 0
  fi
  lcc_out="$("$lcc_cmd" --porcelain </dev/null)" || lcc_rc=$?
  case "$lcc_rc" in
    0) : ;;
    2) die "claude-current refused the launch (exit 2, its reason above): the newest Claude Code installed is behind the one npm publishes. Nothing was written. CLAUDE_ALLOW_STALE=1 $prog … launches it anyway, or CLAUDE_BIN=<path> pins one." 2 ;;
    *) die "claude-current named no Claude Code to launch (exit $lcc_rc, its reason above). Nothing was written." 1 ;;
  esac
  while IFS= read -r lcc_line; do
    case "$lcc_line" in
      path=*)    [ -n "$lcc_path" ]    || lcc_path="${lcc_line#path=}" ;;
      version=*) [ -n "$lcc_version" ] || lcc_version="${lcc_line#version=}" ;;
      status=*)  [ -n "$lcc_status" ]  || lcc_status="${lcc_line#status=}" ;;
    esac
  done <<LCC
$lcc_out
LCC
  case "$lcc_path" in
    /*) [ -f "$lcc_path" ] && [ -x "$lcc_path" ] ||
          die "claude-current answered '$lcc_path', which is not an executable file. Nothing was written." 1 ;;
    *) die "claude-current exited 0 without an absolute path= line (it printed '$lcc_out'). Nothing was written." 1 ;;
  esac
  CLAUDE_BIN="$lcc_path"
  CLAUDE_RESOLVED_BIN="$lcc_path"
  export CLAUDE_BIN CLAUDE_RESOLVED_BIN
  lane_claude_version="$lcc_version"
  lane_claude_checked=1
  step "launching claude ${lcc_version:-of an unknown version} (${lcc_status:-no status}) at $lcc_path, as claude-current chose"
}

LS_TMP=""
LS_REF_TMP=""   # the routing reads' copy of a ref's register (T428), while it lives
cleanup() { [ -n "${LS_TMP:-}" ] && rm -f -- "$LS_TMP"; [ -n "${LS_REF_TMP:-}" ] && rm -f -- "$LS_REF_TMP"; return 0; }
# A SIGNAL ENDS THE RUN, AND `cleanup` RUNS ON THE WAY OUT (REV-ACT2 N2;
# `lane-end`'s form, #138 item 6). `cleanup` returns rather than exits, so the
# one trap `cleanup EXIT INT TERM` ran it and then RESUMED the script at the
# next command: after a ^C or a TERM the act carried on, and a child killed
# with 130 or 143 was read as a verb that failed, which A2 ends with 1. The
# codes are the shell's own, 128 plus the signal, and the EXIT trap does the
# removing once.
trap cleanup EXIT
trap 'exit 130' INT
trap 'exit 143' TERM

note() { printf '%s: %s\n' "$prog" "$*" >&2; }
step() { printf '%s: %s\n' "$prog" "$*" >&2; }
plan() { printf '%s: PLAN %s\n' "$prog" "$*" >&2; }
die()  { printf '%s: %s\n' "$prog" "$1" >&2; exit "${2:-1}"; }
usage() { sed -n -e '1,2d' -e '/^#/!q' -e 's/^# \{0,1\}//' -e p "$RESOLVED"; }
# EVERY ARGUMENT AS ONE SHELL WORD, WITHOUT `printf %q` AND WITHOUT A PIPE —
# measured on the macOS job at `db206db` and `ac0c697`, where three cases of
# this branch's own went red and no other platform could see it. One line, two
# defects:
#
#   * `printf '%q '` RENDERS DIFFERENTLY UNDER BASH 3.2, which is what Apple
#     ships and what that job runs (`/bin/bash` 3.2.57). Bash 4.4 and later
#     give a multi-line argument back as `$'…\n…'` — one readable, runnable
#     word; 3.2 has no such form and escapes every space and every byte of
#     every multi-byte character with a backslash of its own. The one caller
#     that passes a multi-line argument is the `--no-launch` print of a launch
#     carrying the handoff's top block as its first prompt (Amendment 17(c)),
#     and it got back neither a readable line nor a runnable one.
#   * AND THE PIPE THROUGH `sed` MADE THAT FATAL RATHER THAN UGLY. Under
#     `set -o pipefail` this function's status IS the pipeline's, and BSD `sed`
#     in a UTF-8 locale (`LC_ALL=en_US.UTF-8` on that runner) refuses bytes
#     that are not a valid sequence — `RE error: illegal byte sequence` — which
#     is what 3.2's `%q` makes of the em dash every one of these documents is
#     written with. `quoted` is a BARE command in the `--no-launch` branch, so
#     `set -e` ended the command there: exit 1, and an empty stdout.
#
# So the quoting is this file's own, and it is the POSIX one every shell reads
# the same way: a word of safe characters bare, anything else in single quotes
# with its own single quotes spelled `'\''`. A newline inside single quotes IS
# a newline, so the line stays readable as well as runnable, on either bash.
quoted() {
  q_sep=""
  # q_r is a variable, not an inline escape. Inside double quotes bash 3.2, and
  # bash 4.2 and older or BASH_COMPAT up to 42, keep the backslashes of a
  # replacement half: `"${q_a//\'/\'\\\'\'}"` printed `\'\\'\'` for a quote and
  # the line did not read back (lane 4, #165 review). A variable's value is not
  # read again, so every bash gives `'\''`.
  q_r="'\\''"
  for q_a in "$@"; do
    case "$q_a" in
      '' | *[!A-Za-z0-9._/:=@%+,-]*)
        printf "%s'%s'" "$q_sep" "${q_a//\'/$q_r}" ;;
      *) printf '%s%s' "$q_sep" "$q_a" ;;
    esac
    q_sep=' '
  done
  return 0
}

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ arguments

dry_run=0; no_launch=0; estate=""; dir_override=""; confirm=0; take_yes=0
agent_flag=""; fresh_flag=0; op_flag=""
# THE NAME GUARD'S BINDING MODE (openRepoTools#70) — the transcript being bound.
bind_sid=""
# AND ITS RERUN (feature 007, T503, FR-039): `bind_cell_ends` is 1 where the
# published session cell already ENDS on the bound uuid, and `bind_rerun` is 1
# where the lane's binding also names this window and that uuid. Both are set
# in step 3b(ii), below, and read by the three writes of steps 5, 5b and 5c.
bind_cell_ends=0; bind_rerun=0
# AMENDMENT 18(c)/(e) — the three words a second place has for a bound lane.
request_handoff=0; force_why=""; handoff_wait=300
positional=(); pass=()
# THIS RUN'S ARGUMENTS AS THEY WERE GIVEN, before the loop below takes them:
# the `--no-launch` act a resume's arm names under a seq base is this act
# with its own arguments among them, as typed (`ls_q41_line`; lane 3's Q41,
# #165 6780753157, and Q64, #165 6807881675).
LS_ARGV=("$@")

while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case "$1" in
    --dry-run)   dry_run=1; shift ;;
    --no-launch) no_launch=1; shift ;;
    --confirm)   confirm=1; shift ;;
    --yes)       take_yes=1; shift ;;
    # AMENDMENT 17(c) — RESUME THIS LANE FROM ANY AGENT THAT CAN READ IT. A
    # lane's state is model-neutral: the register row, the object log, the
    # handoff with its Rule 3 resume prompt, the worktrees and branches. The
    # transcript is ONE agent's convenience, and until this flag the only door
    # into a lane launched `claude` and nothing else. Both spellings refuse an
    # empty value, for the reason `--dir` states above.
    --agent)     agent_flag="${2-}"; [ -n "$agent_flag" ] || die "--agent needs a name" 2; shift 2 ;;
    --agent=*)   agent_flag="${1#--agent=}"; [ -n "$agent_flag" ] || die "--agent needs a name" 2; shift ;;
    # `--fresh` — THE FRESH-SESSION MODE AS AN ARGUMENT, and it exists because
    # the environment seam it stands beside cannot be relied on to arrive
    # (openRepoTools#94, measured on Eagle 2026-09-15T20:59Z). `/ctx` set
    # `LANE_START_FRESH=1` in the command it respawned the pane with, the
    # launcher re-created the child through `tmux new-session`, and tmux hands
    # a command the SERVER's environment and not the client's — so this
    # command was reached with the variable gone and resumed the very
    # transcript the context clear had just paused. An ARGUMENT survives an
    # `exec`; it does not survive a re-creation either, which is why the
    # DURABLE RESTART INTENT below outranks both. This flag is for the caller
    # that reaches this command directly.
    --fresh)     fresh_flag=1; shift ;;
    # THE OPERATION THIS LAUNCH BELONGS TO (openRepoTools#94). The supervisor
    # passes it so that this run reads the intent it was started for and no
    # other, and so that a launch arriving with a STALE operation refuses
    # rather than consuming a newer lane's intent.
    --operation)   op_flag="${2-}"; [ -n "$op_flag" ] || die "--operation needs an operation id" 2; shift 2 ;;
    --operation=*) op_flag="${1#--operation=}"; [ -n "$op_flag" ] || die "--operation needs an operation id" 2; shift ;;
    # THE NAME GUARD'S BINDING MODE (openRepoTools#70). The guard's choice `2`
    # passes the transcript it is running in, and this run stamps THAT id rather
    # than the one the row would have resumed. What it may be combined with is
    # decided below the loop, where every flag has been read.
    --bind)      bind_sid="${2-}"; [ -n "$bind_sid" ] || die "--bind needs the transcript uuid of the session being bound" 2; shift 2 ;;
    --bind=*)    bind_sid="${1#--bind=}"; [ -n "$bind_sid" ] || die "--bind needs the transcript uuid of the session being bound" 2; shift ;;
    # AMENDMENT 18(c) AND (e) — THE SECOND PLACE'S THREE WORDS, and each of
    # them is an ANSWER to a question this command would otherwise ask. A
    # caller with no terminal on stdin cannot be asked (an agent's is not one),
    # so `--request-handoff` is that `y` written down; `--force` is clause
    # (e)'s override, which is a SECOND invocation and never automatic and
    # which takes the WHY it writes into an append-only log; `--wait` is the
    # clause's own default of 300 seconds, named.
    --request-handoff) request_handoff=1; shift ;;
    --force)     force_why="${2-}"; [ -n "$force_why" ] || die "--force takes the WHY you are taking a lane another place is bound to: it is written into that lane's append-only log and it is the sentence the bound session reads when it refuses every prompt from then on (Amendment 18(e))" 2; shift 2 ;;
    --force=*)   force_why="${1#--force=}"; [ -n "$force_why" ] || die "--force takes the WHY you are taking a lane another place is bound to (Amendment 18(e))" 2; shift ;;
    --wait)      handoff_wait="${2-}"; [ -n "$handoff_wait" ] || die "--wait needs a number of seconds" 2; shift 2 ;;
    --wait=*)    handoff_wait="${1#--wait=}"; [ -n "$handoff_wait" ] || die "--wait needs a number of seconds" 2; shift ;;
    # THE TWO SPELLINGS OF ONE FLAG GIVE ONE ANSWER, and these two did not
    # (#26, the review of `c3ebcfe`, `lane-start:707`). `--dir ""` refuses;
    # `--dir=` set the variable to nothing and fell through as if the operator
    # had not named a directory at all — so the rungs below it ran, and one of
    # them is `$PROJECTS_ROOT/<repo>`: `lane-start --dir= <repo> <n>` started in
    # a checkout the operator had not asked for instead of refusing a path they
    # had not given. `restart:151` — the same flag, on the sibling command —
    # has carried the guard on both spellings all along, which is what makes
    # this a divergence rather than a decision. Held for every flag in this
    # estate by `test_the_two_spellings_of_a_flag_refuse_the_same_empty_value`,
    # derived from the arms themselves rather than from a list.
    --dir)       dir_override="${2-}"; [ -n "$dir_override" ] || die "--dir needs a path" 2; shift 2 ;;
    --dir=*)     dir_override="${1#--dir=}"; [ -n "$dir_override" ] || die "--dir needs a path" 2; shift ;;
    --estate)    estate="${2-}"; [ -n "$estate" ] || die "--estate needs a name" 2; shift 2 ;;
    --estate=*)  estate="${1#--estate=}"; [ -n "$estate" ] || die "--estate needs a name" 2; shift ;;
    -h|--help)   usage; exit 0 ;;
    --)          shift; pass=("$@"); break ;;
    -*)          die "unknown option '$1' — see $prog --help" 2 ;;
    *)           positional+=("$1"); shift ;;
  esac
done

repo=""; n=""; verbatim=""
case "$handoff_wait" in ''|*[!0-9]*) die "--wait takes a whole number of seconds; '$handoff_wait' is not one" 2 ;; esac
# TWO ANSWERS TO ONE QUESTION ARE NOT AN ANSWER. `--request-handoff` asks the
# bound place to release the lane and waits for it; `--force` releases it on
# that place's behalf without asking. Passing both says both, and which one a
# run took would be decided by the order of two arms rather than by the person.
[ "$request_handoff" = 1 ] && [ -n "$force_why" ] &&
  die "--request-handoff ASKS the place that holds this lane to hand it off and waits for it; --force takes it without asking and makes that place stop loudly (Amendment 18(e)). They are two different acts on somebody else's session — pick one." 2
# THE BINDING MODE BINDS A SESSION THAT IS ALREADY RUNNING, AND NOTHING ELSE
# (openRepoTools#70). Its whole reason to exist is that the guard's `yes` ran
# `--no-launch` and the row's resume path stamped `RESUMED by <the id the row
# already had>` — a conversation nobody was in. So every combination that could
# put a different id on that stamp is refused here, before anything is read or
# written: a launch (the session is running; there is nothing to start), a fresh
# session (a second answer to which session this lane is), and another agent
# (the session being bound is the Claude one the guard is running in). These are
# refusals, never quiet corrections: a caller that passes them has misread what
# the mode is for.
if [ -n "$bind_sid" ]; then
  [[ "$bind_sid" =~ ^[0-9a-fA-F]{8}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{12}$ ]] ||
    die "--bind takes the transcript uuid of the session live in this window (the name of its <uuid>.jsonl), and '$bind_sid' is not one" 2
  [ "$no_launch" = 1 ] ||
    die "--bind is the name guard's binding mode: it binds the session ALREADY running in this window and launches nothing, so it is given only with --no-launch (Amendment 12(h) rule 2, openRepoTools#70)" 2
  [ "$fresh_flag" = 0 ] ||
    die "--bind binds the session live in this window and --fresh starts a new one: two answers to which session this lane is. Pick one." 2
  [ -z "$agent_flag" ] ||
    die "--bind binds the Claude session live in this window, and --agent names an agent to launch; a run that launches nothing has no agent to name" 2
fi
case "${#positional[@]}" in
  2) repo="${positional[0]}"; n="${positional[1]}" ;;
  1)
    arg="${positional[0]}"
    if [[ "$arg" =~ ^[0-9]+[A-Za-z]?$ ]]; then
      n="$arg"
    elif [[ "$arg" == *-* ]] && [[ "${arg##*-}" =~ ^[0-9]+[A-Za-z]?$ ]]; then
      repo="${arg%-*}"; n="${arg##*-}"
    elif [ -n "$dir_override" ]; then
      verbatim="$arg"
    else
      # `lane-start <repo>` WITH NO POSITION LISTS AND SUGGESTS (Brett Heap,
      # 2026-09-13, interactive multi-choice, verbatim "Yes, list and suggest
      # (Recommended)"; A11 Addendum 4 ruling 6's third delta, tracked as
      # `opensoft/openRepoTools#28`). It used to refuse with a placeholder `<n>`
      # for the operator to translate, at the one moment they are trying to
      # start work and do not know which positions are taken.
      #
      # IT RENDERS NOTHING OF ITS OWN. `lanes --prefix <repo>` is that listing
      # and its next-free-position footer, installed beside this command by
      # `openRepoTools --install`; a second rendering here is a second thing to
      # drift. Where it is absent — an older workstation — the refusal below is
      # still the whole answer, so this is an improvement and never a dependency.
      # BESIDE THIS COMMAND FIRST, THEN PATH (Copilot round 1 on #45): the two
      # are placed in ONE directory by `openRepoTools --install`, and a
      # workstation carrying two copies must not render this listing out of a
      # different version from the one it is standing in. Same rule as the bare
      # form's `lane` below and as `lane`'s own `$LANES_EDIT`.
      if [ -x "$SCRIPT_DIR/lanes" ]; then ls_cmd="$SCRIPT_DIR/lanes"
      else ls_cmd="$(command -v lanes 2>/dev/null || :)"
      fi
      # AND THE REFUSAL PROMISES A LISTING ONLY WHERE ONE WAS MADE (#26, the
      # review of `c3ebcfe`, `lane-start:602`). `|| :` made every code of that
      # read the same code, and the sentence below then sent a person to "the
      # listing above" when what was above it was `lanes`'s own refusal. `0` and
      # `8` both PRINT one — 8 is the empty repository, whose listing is the
      # next free position and the line that takes it — `2` is a `lanes`
      # predating the read, which says so in its own words, and anything else is
      # a read that FAILED. Nothing of the listing is re-rendered here on any of
      # them: this command renders none of it (the paragraph above), the read's
      # own output and stderr have already gone to the terminal, and what
      # changes is only whether this refusal claims a listing it did not get.
      ls_listed=0
      if [ -n "$ls_cmd" ]; then
        note "'$arg' is a repository and not a lane. Its lanes, and the position that is free:"
        ls_rc=0
        LANES_NO_FETCH=1 "$ls_cmd" --prefix "$arg" >&2 || ls_rc=$?
        case "$ls_rc" in
          0 | 8) ls_listed=1 ;;
          2) : ;;
          *) note "that listing FAILED (exit $ls_rc) — which is not 'this repository has no lanes', so no position is offered from it" ;;
        esac
      fi
      # STILL EXIT 2, AND NOTHING IS LAUNCHED. A listing is what a person asked
      # for; a caller that scripted `lane-start <repo>` expecting a session must
      # not read 0 from a run that started none.
      from_listing=" with a position from the listing above"
      [ "$ls_listed" = 1 ] || from_listing=""
      die "'$arg' is not a lane: a lane is <repo>-<position>, e.g. $arg-1. Run: $prog $arg <n>$from_listing, or name the checkout yourself with --dir <path> to use '$arg' as the lane name" 2
    fi
    ;;
  0)
    # BARE `lane-start` IS `lane` (Amendment 18 Addendum 1 clause (i-4),
    # ratified 2026-09-14T14:05:54Z verbatim "ratify the addendum"): *"Bare
    # `lane-start` — no repository, no position — is an alias of `lane`, so
    # clause (i) as ruled still holds and there is one implementation."*
    #
    # IT RENDERS NOTHING OF ITS OWN, exactly as the `<repo>`-with-no-position
    # rung above renders nothing of its own: `lane` is the listing, the numbered
    # pick and the three branches, installed beside this command by
    # `openRepoTools --install`. Where it is ABSENT — an older workstation — the
    # refusal below is still the whole answer, so this is an improvement and
    # never a dependency.
    #
    # `exec`, NOT A CALL: this command is itself `exec`ed by the launcher, so a
    # pick that ends in a launch must replace this process rather than leave it
    # waiting on a child that has replaced itself.
    if [ -x "$SCRIPT_DIR/lane" ]; then lane_word="$SCRIPT_DIR/lane"
    else lane_word="$(command -v lane 2>/dev/null || :)"
    fi
    if [ -n "$lane_word" ]; then
      # THE OPTIONS ARE NOT DROPPED ON THE FLOOR (Copilot round 1 on #45,
      # `lane-start:661`). This command's parser has already taken them, and an
      # `exec` with none of them turns `--no-launch` — a flag whose whole
      # promise is that nothing starts — into an interactive pick that launches.
      # `--dry-run` and `--dir` mean the same thing in `lane` and go with it;
      # every other flag here is this command's own, has no meaning for a
      # listing that has not been answered yet, and is REFUSED BY NAME rather
      # than ignored.
      lane_argv=("$lane_word")
      [ "$dry_run" = 0 ] || lane_argv+=(--dry-run)
      [ -z "$dir_override" ] || lane_argv+=(--dir "$dir_override")
      # AMENDMENT 18(c)/(e)'s THREE GO WITH IT, for the reason `--dry-run` and
      # `--dir` do: they mean the same thing in `lane`, whose BOUND ELSEWHERE
      # branch is the other door to the same request, and a flag silently
      # dropped is a promise that was not kept.
      [ "$request_handoff" = 0 ] || lane_argv+=(--request-handoff)
      [ -z "$force_why" ] || lane_argv+=(--force "$force_why")
      [ "$handoff_wait" = 300 ] || lane_argv+=(--wait "$handoff_wait")
      ls_drop=""
      [ "$no_launch" = 0 ] || ls_drop="$ls_drop --no-launch"
      [ "$confirm" = 0 ]   || ls_drop="$ls_drop --confirm"
      [ "$take_yes" = 0 ]  || ls_drop="$ls_drop --yes"
      # AND THE VALUE GOES WITH THE FLAG THAT TOOK ONE (Copilot round 4 on #45,
      # `lane-start:690`). `--estate` and `--` are parsed WITH their operands,
      # and a suggested command carrying the flag and not the value is one
      # nobody can run: `lane-start --estate x --yes` printed `lane-start <repo>
      # <n> --estate --yes`, whose `--estate` swallows the `--yes` and names an
      # estate `--yes`. A line printed for a person to type is a line this
      # command has to be willing to have typed back at it.
      # AND EACH VALUE IS QUOTED THE WAY THE SHELL WILL READ IT BACK (Copilot
      # round 7 on #45, `lane-start:698`): `--estate 'team blue'` printed bare
      # is two words when it is typed again, which is a suggested command that
      # does something else. `quoted` is the same helper every other printed
      # command in this file goes through.
      [ -z "$estate" ]     || ls_drop="$ls_drop --estate $(quoted "$estate")"
      [ "${#pass[@]}" = 0 ] || ls_drop="$ls_drop -- $(quoted "${pass[@]}")"
      # AND THE LINE CARRIES THE OPTIONS THAT WERE *KEPT* TOO (Copilot round 8
      # on #45, `lane-start:706`). The remedy was built out of the refused flags
      # alone, so `lane-start --dir /work/repo --estate x` printed `lane-start
      # <repo> <n> --estate x` and the checkout the person had named was gone
      # from the line they were told to type. What is suggested is the WHOLE
      # command they meant, not the half this rung could not take.
      ls_keep=""
      [ "$dry_run" = 0 ]     || ls_keep="$ls_keep --dry-run"
      [ -z "$dir_override" ] || ls_keep="$ls_keep --dir $(quoted "$dir_override")"
      [ "$request_handoff" = 0 ] || ls_keep="$ls_keep --request-handoff"
      [ -z "$force_why" ]    || ls_keep="$ls_keep --force $(quoted "$force_why")"
      [ "$handoff_wait" = 300 ] || ls_keep="$ls_keep --wait $handoff_wait"
      if [ -n "$ls_drop" ]; then
        die "bare \`$prog\` is \`lane\` (Amendment 18 Addendum 1 clause (i-4)), which lists the
    lanes and binds the one you pick — and$ls_drop belongs to a lane that has
    already been named, so there is nothing here for it to apply to. It is
    refused rather than ignored: a flag that is silently dropped is a promise
    that was not kept.
      pick a lane:                    $prog
      that lane, with those options:  $prog <repo> <n>$ls_keep$ls_drop" 2
      fi
      note "no <repo> and no position: this is \`lane\`, which lists the lanes and binds the one you pick (Amendment 18 Addendum 1 clause (i-4))"
      exec "${lane_argv[@]}"
    fi
    usage >&2; die "a lane needs a position: $prog <repo> <n>" 2 ;;
  *) die "too many arguments: $prog <repo> <n>" 2 ;;
esac

[ -n "$verbatim" ] || [[ "$n" =~ ^[0-9]+[A-Za-z]?$ ]] || die "'$n' is not a position — the lane form is <repo>-<position across, left to right>, e.g. 2" 2

# No <repo> anywhere: the cwd's git root names it, and only its basename.
if [ -z "$repo" ] && [ -z "$verbatim" ]; then
  top="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null || :)"
  [ -n "$top" ] || die "no <repo> given and this directory is not a git checkout — run it inside the lane's checkout, or: $prog <repo> $n" 1
  repo="$(basename -- "$top")"
fi

# ------------------------------------ AMENDMENT 15(a): `<repo>` IS A DIRECTORY
#
# **A lane name is one name under any case, and the register row's spelling is
# the one it carries** (ratified 2026-09-14T00:07:18Z). The `<repo>` half of a
# NEW lane's name has no row to take a spelling from, so it takes the CHECKOUT'S
# REAL DIRECTORY NAME — which is the only spelling on this workstation that is a
# fact rather than a habit, and the one every later reader of the lane's `dir`
# and `$PROJECTS_ROOT/<repo>` rung will meet.
#
# TWO DIRECTORIES DIFFERING ONLY BY CASE ARE A REFUSAL NAMING BOTH. On a
# case-insensitive filesystem — macOS — they cannot both exist and this never
# fires; on Linux they can, and choosing one of them is choosing which
# repository the lane is in.
#
# `ls` and ONE awk rather than a `tr` per entry: this sits in front of a launch,
# `$PROJECTS_ROOT` holds a few dozen directories, and the `[ -d ]` below runs
# only for the names that already matched. The `--` stays for the reason
# `lanes-edit.sh`'s twin scan carries in full (`log_files_named_ci`; Copilot
# round 3 on openRepoTools#41, declined with a measurement): BSD `ls` ends its
# options at `--` like every other POSIX utility, and the step line this
# function produces — `repo repocase → repoCase` — is asserted on the macOS job,
# where an `ls` that refused `--` would leave it silent.
projects_dir_matches() {   # <typed repo>
  ls -- "$PROJECTS_ROOT" 2>/dev/null |
    awk -v want="$1" 'BEGIN { w = tolower(want) } tolower($0) == w { print }' |
    while IFS= read -r pdm_m; do
      [ -d "$PROJECTS_ROOT/$pdm_m" ] && printf '%s\n' "$pdm_m"
    done
}
if [ -n "$repo" ] && [ -d "$PROJECTS_ROOT" ]; then
  repo_hits="$(projects_dir_matches "$repo" || :)"
  repo_hit_n=0
  if [ -n "$repo_hits" ]; then repo_hit_n="$(printf '%s\n' "$repo_hits" | grep -c . || :)"; fi
  # THE REFUSAL IS FOR A RUN THAT HAS NO OTHER ANSWER, AND `--dir` IS THE OTHER
  # ANSWER. Its own remedy names `--dir <path>`, so refusing a run that already
  # passed one is refusing the fix it printed: `lane-start --dir /chosen repotwin
  # 1` was rejected before the rung that honours the flag (Copilot round 1 on
  # openRepoTools#41). With the checkout named, the ambiguity that is left is
  # only about the lane-s NAME, which keeps the spelling that was typed and is
  # then overruled by the register row-s a few lines below — so this SAYS what
  # it did not resolve and carries on.
  if [ "$repo_hit_n" -gt 1 ] && [ -z "$dir_override" ]; then
    die "$PROJECTS_ROOT holds $repo_hit_n directories whose names differ only by case for '$repo': $(printf '%s\n' "$repo_hits" | tr '\n' ' ')— a lane name is ONE name under any case (Amendment 15(a)), so there is no spelling of '$repo' this can take without choosing which repository the lane is in. Name the checkout yourself with --dir <path>, or rename one of those directories." 2
  fi
  if [ "$repo_hit_n" -gt 1 ]; then
    note "$PROJECTS_ROOT holds $repo_hit_n directories whose names differ only by case for '$repo': $(printf '%s\n' "$repo_hits" | tr '\n' ' ')— --dir has named the checkout, so this run is not ambiguous about WHERE the lane is. Its NAME keeps the '$repo' you typed, and the register row's own spelling wins over that below (Amendment 15(a))."
  fi
  if [ "$repo_hit_n" = 1 ] && [ "$repo_hits" != "$repo" ]; then
    step "repo $repo → $repo_hits (Amendment 15(a): the checkout's real directory name under $PROJECTS_ROOT)"
    repo="$repo_hits"
  fi
fi

LANE="${verbatim:-$repo-$n}"
[[ "$LANE" =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*$ ]] || die "'$LANE' is not usable as a window and session name (allowed: letters, digits, . _ -)" 2

# The position after this one, for the refusal to name. `10#` because a lane
# may be `-08` and 08 is not a number in base 8.
if [[ "$n" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]; then next_n="$((10#$n + 1))"; else next_n="<the next free position>"; fi

if [ -z "${LANES_FILE:-}" ]; then die "$(lanes_workspace_why)" 1; fi
[ -f "$LANES_FILE" ] || die "no register at $LANES_FILE — the workspace repository $(lanes_ws_yaml) names carries no lanes/LANES.md. \`openRepoTools wip init\` seeds one (it is idempotent); or set LANES_FILE" 1
[ -x "$LANES_EDIT" ] || die "no register writer at $LANES_EDIT — \`lanes-edit.sh\` is installed beside this command by \`openRepoTools --install\`. Run that, or set LANES_EDIT" 1

# AMENDMENT 23 S5 (slice 2b) — W IS FAST-FORWARDED BEFORE IT IS READ, AND AGAIN
# ONCE THIS LAUNCH'S WRITES ARE DONE. Under `LANES_ROUTE_WRITES=1` and the
# default resolution every write of this lane goes through its own register
# checkout, `<W>-lanes/<lane>/`, and W, the readers' copy this command reads
# the lane's row from, moves only by `lanes-edit.sh refresh-workspace` (plan
# §2.6): its fetch, then `merge --ff-only`, and never a reset, a stash, a commit
# or an abort. So it is asked for twice: here, before the first read through W,
# and just before section 6, so that the next reader of W finds this launch's
# own row, log line and stamp. Without the opt-in W is the write checkout, and
# every write's S3 fast-forwards it under its own lock; an explicit
# `LANES_REPO` or `LANES_FILE` is the caller's register and not W. Neither
# refresh runs then, nor in a dry run, which plans it. Every exit is a note: a
# launch is never refused for a read it can do without (Amendment 8(d)).
LS_ROUTED=0
if [ "${LANES_ROUTE_WRITES:-}" = 1 ] && [ "$LANES_REG_DEFAULT" = 1 ]; then LS_ROUTED=1; fi
LS_REFRESH_RC=""
ls_refresh() {   # <when, as words: "before this launch reads it" or "after its writes">
  [ "$LS_ROUTED" = 1 ] || return 0
  if (( dry_run )); then
    plan "$LANES_EDIT refresh-workspace   # W, fast-forwarded $1 (Amendment 23 S5)"
    return 0
  fi
  LS_REFRESH_RC=0
  "$LANES_EDIT" refresh-workspace || LS_REFRESH_RC=$?
  [ "$LS_REFRESH_RC" = 0 ] ||
    note "W was not fast-forwarded $1 (\`$LANES_EDIT refresh-workspace\` exited $LS_REFRESH_RC; its words are above), and the launch goes on: W is the readers' copy, and only a person repairs it (Amendment 23 S5)."
  return 0
}
ls_refresh "before this launch reads it"

# ------------------------- AMENDMENT 15: THE ROW'S SPELLING IS THE LANE'S NAME
#
# The row wins over the directory, and over what was typed: it is what the
# window is renamed to (step 4), what `--name`s every launch branch (step 5a),
# what the row stamp and the object log carry, and what the log file is called.
# `--verbatim` lanes come through here too — `browser-ui-repair` is a row like
# any other and a `lane-start Browser-UI-Repair` is that lane.
#
# ONE RESOLVER, SHARED. `lanes-edit.sh canon-lane` is the same read `lane-end`,
# `lanes` and `restart` make, for the reason clause (h) gives every other read
# in this toolset: an implementation of "which row is this name's" in each
# command is how they would come to disagree about it.
#
# 2 IS TWO ANSWERS AND THE REFUSAL IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS (Copilot round 4 on
# openRepoTools#41). It is the code a helper predating this amendment spends on
# an unknown subcommand — every workstation until adoption reaches it — and it is
# ALSO `canon-lane`'s own two-rows refusal. Carrying on from both was written
# here on the reading that the refusal is one this script makes again forty lines
# below out of its own read of the register; that read is `$LANES_FILE`, THE
# WORKING TREE, and the register this refusal is about is `origin/<branch>`. On a
# checkout that is behind, the pair is published and this file holds one row: the
# local scan passes, and what the operator gets is the stderr-suppressed
# `register-row` read below turning the SAME refusal into a generic exit 1 that
# names neither spelling. So the refusal is asked for by its own words — no old
# helper emits them from an unknown subcommand — and relayed, with the fallback
# for a genuinely old helper left exactly as it was.
#
# FEATURE 007, T402 - THE BUSY, IN-DOUBT AND UNREADABLE CODES AT A READ ARM
# ("The callers' default arms", design Y:5720-5747; spec:663-690; FR-036). On
# a SQL register a read verb can answer 69 (the register could not be read),
# 75 (register busy) or 3 (in doubt), and none of the three is an answer about
# the lane. Where that table has an arm refuse one, `ls_read_refuse` refuses
# with the verb's own code ("pass the code", Y:5722), naming it, before this
# launch makes any write of its own. Where a read's arm already refused, it
# keeps its words and exits with these three codes instead of its own
# (`ls_read_code`); every other code keeps that arm's exit. Two arms are the
# exception. The ownership seam's (`seam_refuse`) exits 1, UNKNOWN, for every
# code but 0 and 8, these three included: an ownership nobody established is
# refused as UNKNOWN, never passed on (S4; DEC-27; research.md ERR-46). And
# `request-handoff`'s arm is a write's (Y:5731) and keeps 1 until T428. No
# read verb emits any of the three in files mode (Y:5746), so neither changes
# anything there.
ls_read_code() {   # <the verb's exit> <the arm's own exit for any other code>
  case "$1" in 69 | 75 | 3) printf '%s\n' "$1" ;; *) printf '%s\n' "$2" ;; esac
}
ls_read_refuse() {   # <the read, as run> <its exit> <what it would have told> [<what this run has done>]
  local lrr_why lrr_retry
  case "$2" in
    75) lrr_why="the register is busy"; lrr_retry="A retry in seconds is safe." ;;
    3)  lrr_why="the register's answer is in doubt"; lrr_retry="A retry in seconds is safe." ;;
    *)  lrr_why="the register could not be read"; lrr_retry="A retry is safe once the register can be read." ;;
  esac
  die "\`$1\` exited $2: ${lrr_why}, so $3 is NOT known, and that is not an answer. This launch refuses rather than carry on without it. ${4:-Nothing was launched, renamed or written.} ${lrr_retry}" "$2"
}
# A REFUSAL AFTER STEP 4's RENAME (lane 3's ruling 6747281902, T402 item 5):
# it gives this window back its name and its automatic-rename, as #138 item
# 3's refusal does (`ls_window_name_back`, defined at step 4), and it names
# what `request-handoff` wrote where it answered 0, which stands whole
# (R-A11-11). None of this launch's own register writes is made by then.
# Under --dry-run nothing was renamed or written.
ls_late_refuse() {   # <the read, as run> <its exit> <what it would have told>
  local llr_done
  if (( dry_run )); then
    llr_done="Nothing was launched, renamed or written."
  else
    ls_window_name_back
    llr_done="${rb_undo}. This launch has written no row, no STARTED or RESUMED line and no stamp, and launched nothing."
    [ -z "$ls_rh_wrote" ] ||
      llr_done="${llr_done} One line of the handoff stands: ${ls_rh_wrote}, written whole to the append-only log (R-A11-11)."
  fi
  ls_read_refuse "$1" "$2" "$3" "$llr_done"
}

# FEATURE 007, T428 - THIS LAUNCH IS AN ACT, AND ITS BASE IS TAKEN FIRST
# (FR-029, FR-035; "The acts and their expectations", design Y:5567-5596;
# `lane-start`'s table, Y:5627-5657). `lanes-edit.sh act-begin` runs before
# the first read that decides anything, `canon-lane` below, and its one line
# is exported as `LANES_ACT` to every verb this launch calls: on primary
# `<act-id>@<seq>:<generation>`, the register's head when the act began, which
# each write checks its `LANES_EXPECT` scopes against, so a change another
# place makes after this read refuses the write with 2 instead of being
# written over; off primary `<act-id>@files`, which no write reads. A read in
# a dry run too. Its stderr is held and shown only where it fails: off
# primary a success says at most the mode warning every later write repeats.
# No base, no launch, read as lane 3's A2 reads it (#165 6774499606): its
# 69, 75 and 3 refuse with the code (G1's first arm). Everything else
# refuses with 1, naming the verb and its code, before anything is renamed
# or written: its 1 or 64, an exit 0 with no line or a malformed one, and
# its 2 too. A 2 comes from a helper older than T410, which reads neither
# variable (Y:5596); another act goes on without a base there, but
# `lane-start` refuses, because no such helper has `start-record`. And
# `LANES_EXPECT` is never exported (Y:5581): an act's scopes ride as a
# prefix on the one call they belong to, so none is inherited; the one a
# caller exported was unset at the top of this file.
# THE LINE'S FORM (Y:5577-5579), read as `lanes-edit.sh`'s `act_parse`
# reads it, with `case` patterns of spelled-out bracket lists:
# `<act-id>@files`, or `<act-id>@<seq>:<generation>`.
ls_act_form() (   # <the line>: 0 where it is a base
  set -f
  laf_h='[0123456789abcdef]'
  laf_id="${1%%@*}"; laf_rest="${1#*@}"
  case "$1" in *@*) : ;; *) return 1 ;; esac
  case "$laf_id" in "" | *[!0123456789abcdef]*) return 1 ;; esac
  [ "${#laf_id}" -eq 32 ] || return 1
  [ "$laf_rest" != files ] || return 0
  case "$laf_rest" in *:*) : ;; *) return 1 ;; esac
  laf_seq="${laf_rest%%:*}"; laf_gen="${laf_rest#*:}"
  case "$laf_seq" in
    0) : ;;
    [123456789]*)
      case "$laf_seq" in *[!0123456789]*) return 1 ;; esac
      [ "${#laf_seq}" -le 18 ] || return 1 ;;
    *) return 1 ;;
  esac
  # shellcheck disable=SC2254  # the generation's pattern is a glob on purpose
  case "$laf_gen" in
    $laf_h$laf_h$laf_h$laf_h$laf_h$laf_h$laf_h$laf_h-$laf_h$laf_h$laf_h$laf_h-$laf_h$laf_h$laf_h$laf_h-$laf_h$laf_h$laf_h$laf_h-$laf_h$laf_h$laf_h$laf_h$laf_h$laf_h$laf_h$laf_h$laf_h$laf_h$laf_h$laf_h) return 0 ;;
  esac
  return 1
)
LS_TMP="$(mktemp)"
ls_act=""; ls_act_rc=0
ls_act="$("$LANES_EDIT" act-begin 2>"$LS_TMP")" || ls_act_rc=$?
if [ "$ls_act_rc" != 0 ]; then
  [ -s "$LS_TMP" ] && cat -- "$LS_TMP" >&2
  case "$ls_act_rc" in
    69 | 75 | 3) ls_read_refuse "$LANES_EDIT act-begin" "$ls_act_rc" "this launch's base, the register's head the act's writes are checked against" ;;
    2) die "\`$LANES_EDIT act-begin\` exited 2 (its words are above), so this launch has no base for its writes. A \`lanes-edit.sh\` with no \`act-begin\` exits 2 for an unknown subcommand, and has no \`start-record\` either, so this launch refuses rather than go on without a base (A2); install the one beside this script. Nothing was launched, renamed or written." 1 ;;
    *) die "\`$LANES_EDIT act-begin\` exited $ls_act_rc (its words are above), so this launch has no base for its writes, and that is not a base. Nothing was launched, renamed or written." 1 ;;
  esac
fi
if ! ls_act_form "$ls_act"; then
  [ -s "$LS_TMP" ] && cat -- "$LS_TMP" >&2
  die "\`$LANES_EDIT act-begin\` exited 0 and printed no single line of base, \`<act-id>@files\` or \`<act-id>@<seq>:<generation>\` (Y:5577-5578), so this launch has no base for its writes. Nothing was launched, renamed or written." 1
fi
export LANES_ACT="$ls_act"
: > "$LS_TMP"

ls_typed="$LANE"   # the name as typed, for `canon-lane --walk` (T428, below)
cl_out=""; cl_rc=0
cl_out="$("$LANES_EDIT" canon-lane "$LANE" 2>/dev/null)" || cl_rc=$?
cl_why=""; cl_pair=0
case "$cl_rc" in 2 | 66) cl_why="$(LANES_NO_FETCH=1 "$LANES_EDIT" canon-lane "$LANE" 2>&1 >/dev/null || :)" ;; esac
case "$cl_why" in *"Amendment 15(d)"*) cl_pair=1 ;; esac
case "$cl_rc" in
  0)
    if [ -n "$cl_out" ] && [ "$cl_out" != "$LANE" ]; then
      step "lane $LANE → $cl_out (Amendment 15: the register row's spelling is canonical)"
      LANE="$cl_out"
    fi ;;
  2)
    if [ "$cl_pair" = 1 ]; then
      printf '%s\n' "$cl_why" >&2
      die "the PUBLISHED register spells lane $LANE two ways (above), so no read and no write under that name is unambiguous and nothing here picks one — Amendment 15(d): merge the newer row's session id(s) into the older row's session cell, in order, remove the newer row in the SAME commit naming both spellings, push, and re-run. Nothing was launched, renamed or written." 2
    fi ;;
  66)
    # AMENDMENT 16(e) — THE ALIAS TABLE COULD NOT BE READ, AND THAT IS NOT A
    # SPELLING QUESTION (Copilot round 4 on openRepoTools#81). `lanes/aliases.tsv`
    # is what makes a lane's OLD name resolve to its row, so a table nobody can
    # read makes a RENAMED lane look like a lane nobody has — and the next line
    # of this command is the one that decides whether to add a row. Carrying on
    # with the typed name there is a SECOND row for a lane that has been running
    # all day, which is the duplicate Amendment 15(a) exists to refuse.
    printf '%s\n' "$cl_why" >&2
    die "lane $LANE's name could not be resolved: ${LANES_EDIT##*/} could not read the lane alias table (above, exit 66). Amendment 16(e) makes a renamed lane's old name resolve through that file for ever, so a name it cannot answer for may be a lane that is already running under another one — and this command would add a second row for it. Fix the read and re-run. Nothing was launched, renamed or written." 2 ;;
  # 007 (T402; Y:5726; spec:663, spec:688): carrying on with the typed name
  # here is a second row for a lane already running under its row's spelling.
  69 | 75 | 3) ls_read_refuse "$LANES_EDIT canon-lane $LANE" "$cl_rc" "the register's spelling of lane $LANE" ;;
  *) note "$LANES_EDIT canon-lane $LANE failed (exit $cl_rc) — carrying on with the name as typed, which the register may spell differently" ;;
esac

# FEATURE 007, T428 - THE NAMES `canon-lane` WALKED, FOR THE ACT'S KEYS
# (Y:5624; lane 3's G2, #165 6770733371). Its answer changes when a row or an
# alias line appears for any name on the walk, not only for the answer, so
# the act lists `row:<n>` and `alias:<n>` for every name `canon-lane --walk`
# (T411) prints for the name this act gave `canon-lane`: that is what
# `alias:L` stands for. Asked only on a primary base, where the keys are
# read, and only where `canon-lane` answered: in files mode no write reads
# LANES_EXPECT (Y:5596), and a launch that carried on with the typed name
# decided on that name alone. A walk that fails is `act-begin`'s failure
# with the same code (G2), read as lane 3's A2 reads it (#165 6774499606):
# its 69, 75 and 3 refuse with the code (G1's first arm), and any other
# code, or an exit 0 that prints no name, refuses with 1, naming the verb
# and its code, before anything is renamed or written: a key left out is a
# change the write would not see. So its 64, a helper with no `--walk`, and
# its 66, the alias table Amendment 16(e) reads and could not, are each
# named, and each exits 1, as the EXIT table's 1 says (REV-LSLE SHOULD 1):
# `canon-lane`'s own 66 above keeps 16(e)'s 2. Its stderr is held, as
# `act-begin`'s is, and shown where it fails.
LS_WALK=""
case "$LANES_ACT" in
  *@files) : ;;
  *)
    if [ "$cl_rc" = 0 ]; then
      lw_rc=0
      : > "$LS_TMP"
      LS_WALK="$("$LANES_EDIT" canon-lane --walk "$ls_typed" 2>"$LS_TMP")" || lw_rc=$?
      if [ "$lw_rc" != 0 ] || [ -z "$LS_WALK" ]; then
        [ -s "$LS_TMP" ] && cat -- "$LS_TMP" >&2
        lw_why="exited $lw_rc (its words are above)"; lw_fix=""
        case "$lw_rc" in
          69 | 75 | 3) ls_read_refuse "$LANES_EDIT canon-lane --walk $ls_typed" "$lw_rc" "which names lane ${LANE}'s spelling was found through" ;;
          0) lw_why="exited 0 and printed no name, where a walk prints the typed name first" ;;
          64) lw_fix=" A \`lanes-edit.sh\` with no \`--walk\` exits 64, its \`canon-lane\` taking one lane; install the one beside this script." ;;
          66) lw_fix=" Its 66 is the lane alias table it could not read, through which Amendment 16(e) resolves a renamed lane's old names; fix that read and re-run." ;;
        esac
        die "\`$LANES_EDIT canon-lane --walk $ls_typed\` ${lw_why}, so the names lane ${LANE}'s spelling was found through are NOT known, and this launch's writes would leave their keys out: a change to one of them would not refuse them (Y:5624).${lw_fix} Nothing was launched, renamed or written." 1
      fi
      : > "$LS_TMP"
    fi ;;
esac

# THE TEXT FOR A WRITE THAT EXITED 3 (T428; design D8, Y:463-469; FR-028;
# review M11), IN T433's FORM, BOTH SENTENCES (lane 3's G3 and shared ruling
# 3, #165 6770733371; tasks:1400 at B12b): D8's words, then the off-primary
# sentence. A 3 is not "written" and not "NOT written": on primary the write
# is in doubt and may never have committed, and the writer prints the rerun
# that settles it (Y:2661-2665); off primary the commit is local and may
# already be on origin. The writer's stderr is kept, and this script prints
# no command or hint of its own for it (Y:5698-5699): a second write by
# another command line could write it twice. Defined here, above its first
# user, W8's arm.
LS_D8="may not be written; run the printed \`LANES_WRITE_ID\` rerun, which writes it once or finds it. Where the writer printed no such rerun (off primary), the commit is local and may already be on origin"
# NO HAND COMMAND OF ITS OWN AFTER A WRITE THAT DID NOT END 0, UNDER A SEQ
# BASE (lane 3's REV-ACT Q4, #165 6774499606, widened by Q11, #165
# 6779688808; Y:5601). A command run by hand without this launch's
# `LANES_ACT` and `LANES_EXPECT` would write unchecked; after a 2, with them
# it would only be refused again. So under a base `<id>@<seq>:<generation>`,
# after any code but 0 and 3, the writer's words above are kept, this script
# prints no command for the write, and an arm that dies says to run it again
# from its start (`LS_Q4`); an arm the launch goes on past names the act
# without its launch instead (`ls_q41_note`, below; Q39, Q41). A 3 keeps the
# writer's rerun (G3), in T433's text (`LS_D8`).
# Under `@files` today's hand commands stand (Y:5596): no write there reads
# either variable. `act-begin`'s 2 never reaches here: `lane-start` refuses
# it (A2). Defined here, above its first user, W8's arm.
ls_q4() {   # <the write's exit>: 0 where no hand command is printed for it
  case "$1" in 0 | 3) return 1 ;; esac
  case "${LANES_ACT:-}" in "" | *@files) return 1 ;; esac
  return 0
}
LS_Q4="No command is printed for it here, since one run by hand without this launch's \`LANES_ACT\` and \`LANES_EXPECT\` would write unchecked. Run \`$prog\` again from its start (Y:5601)"
# BUT NOT "FROM ITS START" WHERE THE ACT GOES ON TO LAUNCH (lane 3's Q39 and
# Q41, #165 6780753157, correcting Q11 for this command): a second
# `lane-start` from its start would start a second launch. So under a seq
# base a resume's write that ended 69 or 75, and W2 to W6's other codes but
# 0 and 3, keep the writer's words above, print no rerun of their own, and
# the stamp and the launch go on as before; the arm names `lane-start
# --no-launch` with the same arguments, run in the launched session's window
# as its first act: the act without its launch (`--no-launch`, the first act
# INSIDE a running session). A 3 keeps the writer's rerun (G3), in `LS_D8`.
# A new row's arms die, and `LS_Q4`'s "from its start" stands for them, a
# 69 or 75 included, as it does for W8's `request-handoff` 2 (lane 3's Q63
# and Q66, #165 6808141050). Under `@files` today's texts stand.
#
# "THE SAME ARGUMENTS" ARE THE ACT'S OWN (lane 3's Q64, #165 6807881675):
# the run's lane words, `--dir`, `--estate`, `--agent` and `--bind`, as
# typed. Amendment 7(b)'s first act, `ls_defer_line` below, is the
# precedent. The line is built from `LS_ARGV`, each word kept one word by
# `quoted`, so it reads back as typed. Every other option is dropped with
# its value, in either spelling, since each shapes only the launch or
# answers what the first run settled: `--fresh` would take the new-session
# branch again, and defer W7 and the stamp again; `--operation` is the
# supervisor's launch's; `--request-handoff`, `--force` and `--wait` are
# answers already acted on, and `--force` is never automatic; `--confirm`
# and `--yes` are the window question the launch took; `--dry-run` writes
# nothing; and a `--no-launch` is not given twice. `--` and what follows it
# go to claude, and this act launches nothing, so they are dropped too.
#
# THE WALK IS THE PARSER'S ABOVE. A two-token flag takes the next word
# whatever it is, so `--dir --` keeps `--` as the directory, and `--force
# --dir` drops `--dir` as the why. Only `--agent`, `--operation`, `--bind`,
# `--force`, `--wait`, `--dir` and `--estate` take a value, and only they
# have a `=` spelling. The parser refuses any other word that starts with
# `-`, or exits on `--help`, before this line can run, so `-*` here is a
# dropped flag, and a word without one is a lane word. A flag added to the
# parser is added here too.
# The patterns are quoted so that the hygiene test's reading of option arms
# (`_value_flag_arms`, `tests/test_repo_hygiene.py`) reads the parser's,
# not these: unquoted, this walk's arm stood in for seven of them.
#
# BUT WHERE NO TRANSCRIPT UUID IS KNOWN, THE ACT IS AMENDMENT 7(b)'s LINE
# (lane 3's Q65, #165 6808141050). There W2 to W6 run as their own verbs,
# and the same run prints 7(b)'s deferred-record note, whose first act is
# `$prog --no-launch $self_args` with the `--agent` it was given. Two lines
# would invite two runs, and a second run writes anew (Y:5182). So the
# line here is `ls_defer_line`, the one that note prints, and the two notes
# name one command. Where a uuid is known, 7(b)'s note does not print, and
# the walk below stands (Q64). `ls_defer_line` is defined here, above both
# users; `self_args`, `AGENT` and `lane_sid_known` are set before either.
ls_defer_line() {
  local ag=""
  if (( AGENT_EXPLICIT )); then ag=" $(quoted --agent "$AGENT")"; fi
  printf '%s --no-launch %s%s' "$prog" "$self_args" "$ag"
}
ls_q41_line() {
  local a val=0 skip=0
  local -a nl
  if [ "${lane_sid_known:-}" = 0 ]; then ls_defer_line; return 0; fi
  nl=(--no-launch)
  for a in ${LS_ARGV[@]+"${LS_ARGV[@]}"}; do
    if (( skip )); then skip=0; continue; fi
    if (( val )); then val=0; nl+=("$a"); continue; fi
    case "$a" in
      "--agent" | "--bind" | "--dir" | "--estate") val=1; nl+=("$a") ;;
      "--agent="* | "--bind="* | "--dir="* | "--estate="*) nl+=("$a") ;;
      "--operation" | "--force" | "--wait") skip=1 ;;
      "--") break ;;
      -*) ;;
      *) nl+=("$a") ;;
    esac
  done
  printf '%s %s' "$prog" "$(quoted "${nl[@]}")"
}
ls_q41_note() {   # <what was not written, naming the verb and its code> <the arm's own sentence on the launch>
  note "$1. No rerun of it is printed here: one run by hand without this launch's \`LANES_ACT\` and \`LANES_EXPECT\` would write unchecked. $2 In the launched session's window, as that session's first act, run this act without its launch (lane 3, #165 6780753157, Q39, Q41):"
  note "  $(ls_q41_line)"
}

repo="${repo:-$LANE}"          # the verbatim form has no <repo> of its own
estate="${estate:-$repo}"

# AMENDMENT 23 S1, S5 (slice 2b) — THE ROW IS READ FROM W, BROUGHT UP TO DATE
# FIRST. Under the opt-in and the default resolution every write of this lane
# goes through its own checkout, and W, which takes none, is fast-forwarded
# above, so W's working tree carries every row that has landed, this lane's
# own included. (Slice 1a read the row from the lane's checkout instead, and
# made that checkout here to do it; it is made at "5. the row" now, where the
# first write needs it.) Where that refresh did not fast-forward W, or in a dry
# run, which runs none, the row is read from `origin/<branch>` as W last
# fetched it, never from a working tree that may be behind; and where W has no
# such ref, from the working tree, said. Without the opt-in, or with a
# caller's `LANES_REPO` or `LANES_FILE`, the register read is `$LANES_FILE`'s
# working tree, as it always was. Held as text, not a temporary file, because
# the `exec` this command ends in runs no exit trap.
LS_ROWS_FROM=file; LS_ROWS_TEXT=""
# FEATURE 007, T428 - THE TWO ROUTING READS OF `origin/<branch>` GO THROUGH THE
# READER VERB (lane 3's ruling 6747281902, T402 point 1; ERR-32; FR-036). This
# one decides new lane against resume, and the duplicate refusal's count below
# picks that refusal's words; on primary neither may decide from the mirror
# (Y-spec:574, scenarios 1 and 3). The ref's text is handed to `register-text
# lanes/LANES.md` as the file that verb reads, a temporary copy named in
# `LANES_FILE` (lane 3's offered route): in files mode the verb prints that
# file's bytes, so the answer is the ref's text exactly as `git show` gave it;
# on primary it prints the register's own image whatever file it is handed
# (T505), so the read is the register's. The copy is removed at once, and by
# the exit trap if the run is stopped between. 0 with the text in
# LS_REF_TEXT; 1 where git could not show the ref, which keeps each caller's
# old fallback; 2 where the verb did not answer, its code in LS_REF_RC.
ls_ref_register_text() {   # <ref>
  LS_REF_TEXT=""; LS_REF_RC=0
  LS_REF_TMP="$(mktemp)" || { LS_REF_TMP=""; return 1; }
  if ! git -C "$LANES_REPO" show "$1:lanes/LANES.md" > "$LS_REF_TMP" 2>/dev/null; then
    rm -f -- "$LS_REF_TMP"; LS_REF_TMP=""
    return 1
  fi
  LS_REF_TEXT="$(LANES_FILE="$LS_REF_TMP" "$LANES_EDIT" register-text lanes/LANES.md)" || LS_REF_RC=$?
  rm -f -- "$LS_REF_TMP"; LS_REF_TMP=""
  [ "$LS_REF_RC" = 0 ] || { LS_REF_TEXT=""; return 2; }
  return 0
}
ls_rows_source() {
  [ "$LS_ROUTED" = 1 ] || return 0
  [ "$LS_REFRESH_RC" != 0 ] || return 0
  lrs_ref="refs/remotes/origin/${LANES_BRANCH:-main}"
  lrs_rc=0
  ls_ref_register_text "$lrs_ref" || lrs_rc=$?
  case "$lrs_rc" in
    0)
      LS_ROWS_TEXT="$LS_REF_TEXT"; LS_ROWS_FROM=ref
      (( dry_run )) || note "so this lane's row is read from origin/${LANES_BRANCH:-main} as W last fetched it, not from W's working tree" ;;
    2)
      die "the register could not be read: \`LANES_FILE=<origin/${LANES_BRANCH:-main}'s copy> $LANES_EDIT register-text lanes/LANES.md\` exited $LS_REF_RC (its words are above). That is NOT an empty register, and this script decides nothing about lane ${LANE}'s row on an answer it did not get: a lane taken for new on it would be a second row. A \`lanes-edit.sh\` with no \`register-text\` exits 2; install the one beside this script. Nothing was launched, renamed or written." "$LS_REF_RC" ;;
    *)
      LS_ROWS_TEXT=""
      note "W has no origin/${LANES_BRANCH:-main} to read the register from, so this lane's row is read from W's working tree, which may be behind origin (Amendment 23 S5)" ;;
  esac
  return 0
}
ls_rows_source
# FEATURE 007, T402 - THE WORKING TREE'S REGISTER IS READ THROUGH THE VERB
# (FR-036; design Y:4924). `rows_for_lane` and `row_names_for_lane` keep their
# awk, and the text it runs over is `lanes-edit.sh register-text
# lanes/LANES.md`, asked once and held, as the ref's text above is. That verb
# prints `$LANES_FILE` as `lanes-edit.sh` resolves it from the environment, so
# the file this command means is passed on the call: in files mode the text
# is then this file's bytes, as `cat` read them. A non-zero answer refuses
# with that code, here, in this shell: it is never an empty register, and
# no row is added and no session is resumed on an answer that was not got.
# The `origin/<branch>` read above goes through the same verb (T428).
ls_register_load() {
  [ "$LS_ROWS_FROM" = file ] || return 0
  lrl_rc=0
  LS_ROWS_TEXT="$(LANES_FILE="$LANES_FILE" "$LANES_EDIT" register-text lanes/LANES.md)" || lrl_rc=$?
  [ "$lrl_rc" = 0 ] ||
    die "the register could not be read: \`LANES_FILE=$LANES_FILE $LANES_EDIT register-text lanes/LANES.md\` exited $lrl_rc (its words are above). That is NOT an empty register, and this script decides nothing about lane ${LANE}'s row on an answer it did not get: a lane taken for new on it would be a second row. A \`lanes-edit.sh\` with no \`register-text\` exits 2; install the one beside this script. Nothing was launched, renamed or written." "$lrl_rc"
  return 0
}
# The register text the row is counted in: the one source above, for both reads.
ls_register_text() {
  printf '%s\n' "$LS_ROWS_TEXT"
}

# ----------------------------------------- 1b. THE LANE'S DIRECTORY
#
# AMENDMENT 11 CLAUSE (c). A lane's checkout is known to every process that is
# IN it and to nothing that is about to start one, and Evidence 2 turned that
# into a restart that printed `[exited]`: `openXfactory-5`'s checkout is
# `~/projects/xFactory/openxFactory`, this script derived `$PROJECTS_ROOT/<repo>`
# and died here, and the launcher had no `--dir` to pass. Evidence 3 made it
# worse and quieter — a restart started from `/workspace` came up WITHOUT the
# repository's `CLAUDE.md` and without the lane's memory, because the harness
# keys a session to the directory it was started in, while `--resume <uuid>`
# went on continuing the transcript under the repository's own project folder.
# So the directory is READ FROM THE LANE'S OWN RECORD, and this script `cd`s
# into it before it launches (step 6).
#
# SIX RUNGS, FIRST ANSWER WINS, and clause (c) states them here rather than
# leaving them to an implementation because the launcher draft that read the
# first text of that clause invented one of its own:
#
#   1. `--dir <path>` — the operator's word. NOT tested for existence at this
#      rung: the refusal below names the path and the flag that fixes it, and a
#      script that silently dropped what it was told would hide that.
#   2. the SWAP RECORD's `dir` for this lane — `swapped`'s fourth tab-separated
#      field (clause (h)).
#   3. the LANE'S LOG — `lanes-edit.sh lane-dir <lane>`, the `dir ` sub-field of
#      its last lane-kind line carrying one.
#   4. `$PROJECTS_ROOT/<repo>` — the default, and ONLY the default for a lane
#      that has never been started under this amendment.
#   5. THE ESTATE'S `project.yaml` LEGS, and
#   6. A CHECKOUT NAMED FOR THE HOME'S REPOSITORY one or two levels under
#      `$PROJECTS_ROOT` — both added for Evidence 7, both PROVED by the
#      candidate's own `origin` against the lane's recorded home, and both
#      STATED IN FULL beside the code that implements them a hundred lines
#      below rather than a second time here.
#
# THEY WERE FOUR UNTIL EVIDENCE 7 AND THIS BLOCK WENT ON SAYING SO (#26, the
# review of `c3ebcfe`, `lane-start:780`). `708395e` added rungs 5 and 6 to this
# file; `2f44da0` corrected the MANUAL's count to six. The contract THE CODE
# CARRIES still said "FOUR RUNGS" and "THERE IS NO FIFTH RUNG" a hundred lines
# above the two rungs it had grown — which makes the code below look
# unreachable to its next reader, and the one act that reading invites is
# deleting it.
#
# WHAT THAT SENTENCE RULED OUT IS UNCHANGED, AND IT WAS NEVER A COUNT: THE
# CWD'S OWN CHECKOUT IS RULED OUT BY NAME. Deriving the directory from the
# directory the operator happens to be standing in is an inference, and this
# script writes the lane's HOME into its Amendment 7 `STARTED` line from that
# directory's `origin`, which every `#n` the lane afterwards writes inherits. An
# inference that can silently re-home a lane is not worth the refusal it saves;
# the refusal names `--dir`, and `--dir` is one word. Rungs 5 and 6 are not that
# inference and cannot decay into it: every candidate is PROVED by its own
# `origin` against the recorded home, one that fails the proof is passed over,
# and both are skipped entirely for a lane with no recorded home to prove it
# against.
#
# EVERY READ FAILS TO THE NEXT RUNG AND NEVER TO A REFUSAL. Amendment 7(d)'s
# fail-closed rule says a read that could not run must never be taken for
# "nothing to report"; it does not say the rungs below it stop answering. 8 is
# no answer, 2 is a helper predating Amendment 11 — expected and silent, told
# from a caller's bug by the helper's own words rather than by its status —
# and anything else is named once and then carried past.
# `owner/repo` out of a checkout's `origin`. DEFINED HERE rather than beside
# its first old caller, because Evidence 7's two rungs are the FIRST callers
# and they run four hundred lines earlier; a function defined after its call
# site is a function that is not there yet.
origin_nwo() {
  local url nwo
  url="$(git -C "$1" remote get-url origin 2>/dev/null || :)"
  [ -n "$url" ] || return 1
  nwo="$(printf '%s\n' "$url" | sed -E 's#\.git$##; s#^git@[^:]+:##; s#^ssh://[^/]+/##; s#^https?://[^/]+/##')"
  [[ "$nwo" =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+/[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+$ ]] || return 1
  printf '%s\n' "$nwo"
}

# A CANDIDATE IS PROVED BY ITS OWN `origin`, NEVER BY ITS NAME. Both of the two
# rungs Evidence 7 adds hand every candidate through here, and a candidate whose
# origin is not the lane's recorded home is not taken — which is what keeps them
# rungs rather than the inference clause (c) rules out by name.
lane_dir_is_home() {   # <candidate dir> <owner/repo the record names>
  ldh_o="$(origin_nwo "$1" 2>/dev/null || :)"
  [ -n "$ldh_o" ] || return 1
  ldh_c="$("$LANES_EDIT" resolve-repo "$ldh_o" 2>/dev/null || :)"
  [ -n "$ldh_c" ] && ldh_o="$ldh_c"
  [ "$(printf '%s' "$ldh_o" | tr 'A-Z' 'a-z')" = "$(printf '%s' "$2" | tr 'A-Z' 'a-z')" ]
}

DIR=""; DIR_SOURCE=""; DIR_DEFAULT_TRIED=""
if [ -n "$dir_override" ]; then
